| 1 | 9 | Fit to move. *How hard am I working, and how do I keep good form for a whole session at my own pace?* | 13 | g9-aerobic-form; g9-effort-heart-rate; g9-courtesy-aware | SHAPE.S1.12.5; SHAPE.S2.12.9; SHAPE.S2.12.10; SHAPE.S3.12.1; SHAPE.S3.12.2 | Cues: "tall posture, quiet feet, steady breath"; "count ten seconds, times six"; "rate it, then adjust". Settings: alone at own pace, then interval and circuit sessions with a partner | Correct form in endurance training: jogging or brisk walking, rope, a step or dance workout, with posture, stride or cadence and breathing, kept across a continuous session at the student's own pace (S1.12.5). Rate felt effort on the effort scale and adjust it (S2.12.9); resting and working heart rate by a ten-second count scaled to a minute, a working zone found from an age-based estimate and felt effort, and recovery, used to train safely (S2.12.10; FITT used for an endurance session). Awareness of how others feel and think during activity, and courtesy, respect and teamwork in partner and group sessions (S3.12.1, S3.12.2; team-talk and safe-space in use). Opens with retrieval on grade 8's fitness and dance sessions, its test-and-track records and its leading row; readiness check on grade 7's effort scale and FITT in the last ten minutes of day 1. Out: maximal tests and race times (none in this course). Ends: 2 check days (skill task: form kept across a twelve-minute session at own pace, watched in rotation; knowledge task: unseen items, pick a zone for a goal, check it and adjust; conduct: a courtesy behavior named on the card, such as making space for a slower partner), 1 respond day. Tools: printed cue sheets, an interval chart, a clock or timer. Curio Class writes the cue sheet, the effort scale card and the zone worksheet | First because every later training unit uses form, effort and heart rate, and the class norms of courtesy and awareness are set before partner work; endurance form needs no equipment and suits the first weeks outdoors | Candidate: a private twelve-minute "same route, same effort" log the student repeats in unit 6 | Heart rate and effort records are private, never graded or compared; a student with a heart or breathing condition follows the nurse's and family's guidance and may work by felt effort only. No race, no timed run for a grade. Water breaks and shade outdoors. Sensitivity read sources: the federal physical activity guidelines' adolescent pages; the national heart institute's page on physical activity and the heart. Cost: $10 (a wall timer; ropes from the closet) |
| 2 | 9 | Strong and safe. *How do I train strength with good form, and how do I help a partner do the same?* | 11 | g9-strength-form; g9-encourage-feedback | SHAPE.S1.12.6; SHAPE.S3.12.3; SHAPE.S3.12.4 | Cues: "brace, control down, full range"; "one cue, one fix". Settings: body-weight and band stations in pairs, a partner watching with the cue sheet | Correct form in strength and muscle endurance training: squat, push-up (with an incline or knee version), row with a band, lunge and plank, at a controlled speed through a full range, and what a repetition and a set are (S1.12.6). Encourage and support others (S3.12.3) and give specific feedback on a partner's form without being asked, from the cue sheet (S3.12.4). Opens with retrieval on grade 8's fitness sessions and leading, and on unit 1's FITT and effort scale. Out: loaded lifts and maximal lifts (none in this course); spotting is taught only as "a trained spotter for any loaded lift". Ends: 2 check days (skill task: five exercises with form against the cue sheet; knowledge task: name the cue a pictured exercise misses and its fix; conduct: gives one specific piece of feedback unprompted, watched in the pair task), 1 respond day. Tools: resistance bands, mats; Curio Class writes the exercise cue sheets | After unit 1 because strength sessions use its effort and FITT words, and courtesy comes before peer feedback; strength form precedes any training plan (units 6, 8) | Candidate: partner-made cue card for one exercise | Safety read (resistance training). No max lifts; bands checked for wear; floor exercises on mats (Required access). A student with a back, shoulder or joint concern uses the alternative the nurse names. No body talk; strength is about form and function, never looks. Cost: $90 (15 resistance bands) |
| 3 | 9 | Lifetime sports. *What are the key skills of a sport I could play for life, and how can force and rotation make my swing better?* | 13 | g9-lifetime-sport-skills; g9-force-motion | SHAPE.S1.12.1; SHAPE.S2.12.13 | Cues: volleyball forearm pass "platform, angle, legs lift"; target golf swing "grip, rotate, follow through". Settings: drills, then a small-sided net game and a target course | The key skills of two lifetime sports from two families, each in drills and a small game (S1.12.1). Picks: a net sport, volleyball, with a light trainer ball (forearm pass, set, serve, a three-a-side game); a target sport, target golf with plastic clubs and foam balls (grip, stance, swing to a target, a short course on the field). Use ideas of force, motion and rotation to check and improve a skill: leverage and a longer lever in the swing, rotation of hips and shoulders, center of mass in the ready position (S2.12.13), analyzing a partner's or a recorded try and naming one change. Opens with retrieval on grade 8's net and wall, striking and fielding and invasion play; readiness check on grade 6's skill analysis in the last ten minutes of unit 2's respond day. Out: formulas and calculation of force (science's model, qualitative here). Ends: 2 check days (skill task: key parts of two activities against the cue sheet; knowledge task: unseen items, analyze a pictured swing and name a change with a force or rotation reason; conduct: rotates roles and keeps score kindly in the small game, S3.12.2 review), 1 respond day. Tools: trainer balls, plastic clubs, foam balls, cones as targets; Curio Class writes the cue sheets and the analysis card | After units 1 and 2 because sport drills need the warm-up, effort and form habits; force and motion sits with the swing and pass, where leverage and rotation show; before the mid-year check so it can sample S1 and S2 | Candidate: a self-check "before and after one change" on the swing | Safety read (striking implements). Swing space cleared on every side, one swinger per lane, everyone hitting one direction, foam balls only. Recorded tries optional (Paper path). Cost: $240 (8 trainer balls, 15 plastic clubs and foam balls; beach balls on the low-equipment path) |
