76: - **Base text.** None. Physical education is Curio Class's own. Cue sheets, task cards, plan and log templates and
77:   knowledge cards are ours; the appropriate practice guidelines (2009) and PECAT are read into our words, never
78:   quoted. Each cultural game or dance is named only after its own source (the community's own organization or a
79:   public folklife archive) says it is open to be taught (Cory, 2026-11-20).
80: - **Day kinds.** As docs/GLOSSARY.md (row "teaching day") and APPROACH.md "Kinds of day": Understanding (a skill or
81:   concept shown and named in two or three cues, then tried at once, first try watched by the teacher); Fluency (many
82:   tries with feedback on one cue: form stations, partner drills, task cards, no lines); Application (the skill in a
83:   changing setting: a small-sided game, a dance, a training session, a route, a plan used and logged; drafting a plan
84:   or a reflection is Application); Check; Respond. In grades 9 and 10 Fluency is about a third of each unit's teaching
85:   days (form drills before any loaded or timed work); Application is the largest share. In grades 11 and 12 Fluency
86:   is small (form checks inside the student's own sessions) and most days are Application.
87: - **The C3 column** holds, for PE, the unit's cues and the settings it moves through (alone, partner, station,
88:   small-sided game, session, route or event), as science's holds its practices.
89: - **Assessment.** Each unit's first skill day has a baseline watch of the unit's main cue in rotating groups (met,
90:   partly, not yet); its end-of-unit assessment repeats that cue as the skill task, adds a knowledge task (a short
91:   written task of unseen items, an exit-ticket length answer, or a scored product) and marks conduct once, against the
92:   S3 behavior the unit's Ends names (recorded during practice). Growth is the change against the student's own
93:   baseline, never a rank. Units of 10 days or fewer end with one check day; longer units with two, so the teacher sees
94:   every student (six groups of five, about five minutes a group while the others work at stations or write the
95:   knowledge task). A product unit (a plan, a log, a case, a reflection) counts its draft day, written feedback and
96:   revision before the score inside its own days and says so in its Ends; plans, logs and fitness records are scored on
97:   what the student planned, recorded and changed, never on fitness results. No score comes from a day a skill is first
98:   taught. A respond day follows each unit check; the next unit's instant activity also takes the "not yet" cue.
99: - **Readiness and retrieval.** Each assumed row's grade and course of last use is named in Seams and in the unit that
100:   needs it. A row used the year before (grade 8 rows in grade 9; grade 9 in 10; grade 10 in 11; grade 11 in 12) opens
101:   as a retrieval task inside a day, in the instant activity of the first skill day that needs it. A row last used two
102:   or more grades back (grade 6 and 7 rows in grade 9; grade 7 and 8 rows in grade 10) gets a readiness check of ten
103:   minutes before the unit opens: the last ten minutes of the previous unit's respond day, or of the mid-year respond
104:   day between the two units; units 1 and 7 (first in their year) take the last ten minutes of day 1, after the hook.
105:   Day 1 of every other unit stays clean for the hook and the question. The count starts from a student who took Physical
106:   Education, grades 6 to 8, and takes the years in order. A grade 11 elective taken in grade 12 meets its grade 10 rows
107:   two grades back: those retrieval tasks become readiness checks. A grade 12 student who did not take grade 11 gets a
108:   readiness check on the grade 10 row behind each grade 11 row the unit assumes (named in each grade 12 unit), and the
109:   unit's first Understanding day teaches the grade 11 step as a bridge (rule 32). The scope gate's count governs.
110: - **Space, weather and equipment.** Every unit runs in a gym, on a field or in a cleared room, with a low-equipment
111:   version (resistance bands or body weight for any weights, beach balls or balloons for trainer balls, tape lines and
112:   cones, paper maps) and a wet-weather version named in the unit plan (bar criterion 5). Class of thirty: stations in
113:   groups of four or five, one item a pair or better. Outdoor days carry a water break, shade and sun protection in warm
114:   weather, and the school's cold and heat rules; the unit plan names them (APPROACH.md, "Safe space").
115: - **Required access.** What the low-equipment path cannot replace: mats or another soft surface for floor strength
116:   work, stretching and landings (units 2, 7, 14, 19: mats from the school's stock, carpet or dry grass; on a hard floor
117:   with none, those tasks are done standing or wait for a dry outdoor day); real life jackets for unit 11's fastening
118:   task (borrowed or bought); a safe outdoor route on or next to school grounds for unit 10 (an indoor map course is the
119:   wet-weather and no-route version, and an off-site trip runs only where the school's trip policy allows it). Each
120:   unit's last column names what it needs, the safe substitute and the purchase if none is available.
121: - **Supplies and cost.** Provisional cost per unit for a class of 30, counting reusable items once, if the school has
122:   no PE equipment at all: shown in each unit's last column. Across the band about $780 (grade 9 about $555, grade 10
123:   about $205, grades 11 and 12 about $20), almost all of it in reusable gear a PE closet usually holds; a school with an
124:   ordinary PE closet spends little. The low-equipment path's listed buys and the Required access items are provisional,
125:   so the path is not promised at no cost (For the lead 5).
126: - **Devices.** None per student. One shared teacher device is optional: it shows cue pictures or a short skill video,
127:   runs an interval timer and plays music for dance and aerobic days (units 1, 5, 12, 17). One owned code needs
128:   technology: SHAPE.S2.12.15 (unit 8), where the student examines and uses a technology that supports an active life.
129:   No tool is required for it: a student with no device of their own, or who chooses not to use one, uses a non-screen
130:   technology (a stopwatch or wall clock with a paper interval chart, or a paper pedometer count kept by hand) for the
131:   same two weeks and judges what it showed against the same questions, so every student meets the code in full. A
132:   student's own device is used only with the student's choice, and no data leaves the student (APPROACH.md, "What a
133:   tool may and may not do").
134: - **Paper path.** Every day runs from printed cue sheets, task cards, plan and log templates and the teacher's record
135:   sheet; no tool is needed, no student account exists, and no tool judges movement, ranks students or collects fitness
136:   data. Recorded skills for analysis (units 3, 14, 19) are optional: a partner watching with the cue sheet is the paper
137:   path, and any recording stays on the school's device and is deleted after the lesson.
138: - **Reading load.** Cue sheets and task cards are picture-led with short lines at a grade 9 reading level; knowledge
139:   tasks are short. Each unit names its movement words (the rows' `words`), taught with a picture or demonstration and
140:   a sentence frame ("My knees track over my ___."). A student slow to say or write a cue may show it; the check marks
141:   movement and understanding apart, so a language gap is not marked as a skill gap.
142: - **Supports.** Every lesson, closure and check task has an adaptation for disabilities and a range of skill and
143:   fitness (equipment size and load, distance, speed, rules, roles). A student with a short-term injury or a medical
144:   condition follows the nurse's and family's guidance, with a moving role for the parts that are fine or a seated
145:   role (cue watcher, timer, coach with the cue sheet, keeper of their own log). Groups are made by the teacher, quickly
146:   and privately. No exercise as punishment, no elimination, no human targets, no public team picks, no activity split
147:   by gender. Extension is the same cue in a harder setting, never the next year's work.
148: - **Water safety.** Dry land only, for every school: unit 11 teaches fastening a life jacket, a reach rescue and a
149:   throw rescue to a target, cold-water and current risks and what to do in an emergency, and what a survival float
150:   and a stroke are, shown on the floor. In-water tasks belong to a school's own aquatics program with a qualified
151:   lifeguard on site and the school's swim policy; no student is made to enter water (Cory, 2026-11-20).
152: - **Swaps and leave-outs.** A school or family may swap a named cultural game or dance (units 5, 9, 22) for one whose
153:   source says it is open to be taught, or an outdoor activity that does not fit its place (unit 10) for another
154:   outdoor activity the place offers (STANDARD.md 5). A unit left out takes its owned codes off the course page's claim;
155:   no other unit claims them. A student 18 or over gets the family note and makes their own leave-out or consent choice
156:   (Cory, 2026-11-22).
157: - **Shorter path.** The 64-session path each year (APPROACH.md, "Pacing") drops 16 of the year's 80 days: practice days
158:   only (Fluency days and repeated small-game or session days that use a skill already taught), at most 3 in any unit,
159:   never a unit's last Fluency day before its check, and never a check, respond, baseline, first-teaching, readiness,
160:   product draft, feedback or revision day; both course-level checks and every respond day stay. Days are dropped by
161:   practice load, not evenly. The scope and sequence names the 16 days a year and confirms each unit keeps at least one
162:   Fluency or practice day and every owned outcome stays taught and checked.
163: - **Grade 12 calendar.** Grade 12 teaches to its last week, but its year-end check sits in week 28 (course days 68 and
164:   69, respond day 70), so a senior who leaves early has finished it. Unit 24 (the look back, weeks 29 to 32) follows.
165:   If seniors leave before week 31, unit 24 folds to its short form: its first teaching day, one draft day with written
166:   feedback, and the scored reflection (3 days); the other 7 days fold, and the reflection draws on the portfolio pieces
167:   kept through the year.
168: - **Logic model.** One page a year in the scope and sequence (inputs: 80 sessions of 45 minutes, the cue sheets, plan
169:   templates and the equipment list; activities: the units; outcomes: the grade outcomes per standard above); the
170:   course page says the course has not been tried with students and claims no effect on fitness or health (APPROACH.md,
171:   "Sources").
172: - **Sensitive ground and teacher notes.** Every PE teacher page carries health's disclosure steps word for word
173:   (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"), and changing clothes follows the school's
174:   policy with no one changing in front of others (Cory, 2026-11-21). No weighing, no body measurements, no body weight,
175:   calorie or diet talk; fitness and heart-rate records are the student's own and private, never graded, posted or
176:   compared; a grade never rests on dress, attendance, effort alone or a fitness result. PE does not treat mental health
177:   conditions; the teacher page names the school counselor (units 12, 18, 21, 24).
178: - **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md);
179:   no outside human reader is planned (settled 2026-11-14). Units with cultural content (5, 9, 22) name the communities
180:   whose games and dances they use and, before the read, the agent studies that community's own published sources on
181:   the activity and on whether it is open to be taught; the agent's read confirms it is open to be taught before a unit
182:   names it (each pick's spot-check is a browser check for the lead, For the lead 3). Units with body, food, emotion or
183:   heart-rate content (1, 8, 10, 12, 18, 20, 21, 23, 24) and disability adaptations in every unit name their sources in
184:   the last column.
185: - **Safety read.** The safety agent reads, before they go live, every unit with resistance training, striking tools,
186:   plyometrics, mats or an outdoor activity (units 2, 3, 4, 7, 10, 14, 19, 20) and the water unit (11), against these
187:   free guides: the Nova Scotia Department of Education's "Physical Education Safety Guidelines, Grades Primary-12"
188:   (September 2021,
189:   https://curriculum.novascotia.ca/sites/default/files/documents/resource-files/Physical%20Education%20Safety%20Guidelines%20%282021%29%20EN.pdf;
190:   opened 2026-11-25 by the K to 2 frame writer); SPHEReS ("School Physical Activity, Health and Education Resource for
191:   Safety", https://myspheres.ca/en/), the University of Alberta Injury Prevention Center's online guidelines for
192:   Alberta schools (home page opened 2026-11-25 by the K to 2 frame writer); and for water CDC's "Preventing Drowning"
193:   page (https://www.cdc.gov/drowning/prevention/index.html, opened 2026-11-25, dated 2025-09-29). The safety agent
194:   reads the sections each unit needs (fitness and weight training, striking implements, gymnastics mats, outdoor
195:   pursuits such as orienteering, hiking and cycling, aquatics). No student sign-off form. Answer keys get the
196:   answer-key agent's read.
