302: | 1 | 9 | Fit to move. *How hard am I working, and how do I keep good form for a whole session at my own pace?* | 13 | g9-aerobic-form; g9-effort-heart-rate; g9-courtesy-aware | SHAPE.S1.12.5; SHAPE.S2.12.9; SHAPE.S2.12.10; SHAPE.S3.12.1; SHAPE.S3.12.2 | Cues: "tall posture, quiet feet, steady breath"; "count ten seconds, times six"; "rate it, then adjust". Settings: alone at own pace, then interval and circuit sessions with a partner | Correct form in endurance training: jogging or brisk walking, rope, a step or dance workout, with posture, stride or cadence and breathing, kept across a continuous session at the student's own pace (S1.12.5). Rate felt effort on the effort scale and adjust it (S2.12.9); resting and working heart rate by a ten-second count scaled to a minute, a working zone found from an age-based estimate and felt effort, and recovery, used to train safely (S2.12.10; FITT used for an endurance session). Awareness of how others feel and think during activity, and courtesy, respect and teamwork in partner and group sessions (S3.12.1, S3.12.2; team-talk and safe-space in use). Opens with retrieval on grade 8's fitness and dance sessions, its test-and-track records and its leading row; readiness check on grade 7's effort scale and FITT in the last ten minutes of day 1. Out: maximal tests and race times (none in this course). Ends: 2 check days (skill task: form kept across a twelve-minute session at own pace, watched in rotation; knowledge task: unseen items, pick a zone for a goal, check it and adjust; conduct: a courtesy behavior named on the card, such as making space for a slower partner), 1 respond day. Tools: printed cue sheets, an interval chart, a clock or timer. Curio Class writes the cue sheet, the effort scale card and the zone worksheet | First because every later training unit uses form, effort and heart rate, and the class norms of courtesy and awareness are set before partner work; endurance form needs no equipment and suits the first weeks outdoors | Candidate: a private twelve-minute "same route, same effort" log the student repeats in unit 6 | Heart rate and effort records are private, never graded or compared; a student with a heart or breathing condition follows the nurse's and family's guidance and may work by felt effort only. No race, no timed run for a grade. Water breaks and shade outdoors. Sensitivity read sources: the federal physical activity guidelines' adolescent pages; the national heart institute's page on physical activity and the heart. Cost: $10 (a wall timer; ropes from the closet) |
303: | 2 | 9 | Strong and safe. *How do I train strength with good form, and how do I help a partner do the same?* | 11 | g9-strength-form; g9-encourage-feedback | SHAPE.S1.12.6; SHAPE.S3.12.3; SHAPE.S3.12.4 | Cues: "brace, control down, full range"; "one cue, one fix". Settings: body-weight and band stations in pairs, a partner watching with the cue sheet | Correct form in strength and muscle endurance training: squat, push-up (with an incline or knee version), row with a band, lunge and plank, at a controlled speed through a full range, and what a repetition and a set are (S1.12.6). Encourage and support others (S3.12.3) and give specific feedback on a partner's form without being asked, from the cue sheet (S3.12.4). Opens with retrieval on grade 8's fitness sessions and leading, and on unit 1's FITT and effort scale. Out: loaded lifts and maximal lifts (none in this course); spotting is taught only as "a trained spotter for any loaded lift". Ends: 2 check days (skill task: five exercises with form against the cue sheet; knowledge task: name the cue a pictured exercise misses and its fix; conduct: gives one specific piece of feedback unprompted, watched in the pair task), 1 respond day. Tools: resistance bands, mats; Curio Class writes the exercise cue sheets | After unit 1 because strength sessions use its effort and FITT words, and courtesy comes before peer feedback; strength form precedes any training plan (units 6, 8) | Candidate: partner-made cue card for one exercise | Safety read (resistance training). No max lifts; bands checked for wear; floor exercises on mats (Required access). A student with a back, shoulder or joint concern uses the alternative the nurse names. No body talk; strength is about form and function, never looks. Cost: $90 (15 resistance bands) |
304: | 3 | 9 | Lifetime sports. *What are the key skills of a sport I could play for life, and how can force and rotation make my swing better?* | 13 | g9-lifetime-sport-skills; g9-force-motion | SHAPE.S1.12.1; SHAPE.S2.12.13 | Cues: volleyball forearm pass "platform, angle, legs lift"; target golf swing "grip, rotate, follow through". Settings: drills, then a small-sided net game and a target course | The key skills of two lifetime sports from two families, each in drills and a small game (S1.12.1). Picks: a net sport, volleyball, with a light trainer ball (forearm pass, set, serve, a three-a-side game); a target sport, target golf with plastic clubs and foam balls (grip, stance, swing to a target, a short course on the field). Use ideas of force, motion and rotation to check and improve a skill: leverage and a longer lever in the swing, rotation of hips and shoulders, center of mass in the ready position (S2.12.13), analyzing a partner's or a recorded try and naming one change. Opens with retrieval on grade 8's net and wall, striking and fielding and invasion play; readiness check on grade 6's skill analysis in the last ten minutes of unit 2's respond day. Out: formulas and calculation of force (science's model, qualitative here). Ends: 2 check days (skill task: key parts of two activities against the cue sheet; knowledge task: unseen items, analyze a pictured swing and name a change with a force or rotation reason; conduct: rotates roles and keeps score kindly in the small game, S3.12.2 review), 1 respond day. Tools: trainer balls, plastic clubs, foam balls, cones as targets; Curio Class writes the cue sheets and the analysis card | After units 1 and 2 because sport drills need the warm-up, effort and form habits; force and motion sits with the swing and pass, where leverage and rotation show; before the mid-year check so it can sample S1 and S2 | Candidate: a self-check "before and after one change" on the swing | Safety read (striking implements). Swing space cleared on every side, one swinger per lane, everyone hitting one direction, foam balls only. Recorded tries optional (Paper path). Cost: $240 (8 trainer balls, 15 plastic clubs and foam balls; beach balls on the low-equipment path) |
305: | 4 | 9 | Backyard games and tactics. *What makes a backyard game fun to play well, and which tactic wins the point?* | 12 | g9-casual-rec; g9-lifetime-tactics | SHAPE.S1.12.2; SHAPE.S2.12.1; SHAPE.S2.12.2 | Cues: disc "edge flat, snap, follow through"; badminton "ready, reach, high to low"; tactics "where is the space?". Settings: drills, then doubles games and round-robin stations a family could set up | The key moves of casual recreation and backyard games with equipment a family can own cheaply: a flying disc (backhand throw and catch, a short disc target course), a bean-bag toss (underhand toss to a board), and badminton (serve, clear, drop) (S1.12.2). Tactics in the lifetime sports of unit 3 (volleyball positioning, target golf shot choice) (S2.12.1) and in these games (badminton depth and the weak side, disc target lines) (S2.12.2), each explained and then used in a game. Opens with retrieval on grade 8's net and wall and invasion play and its net-wall and striking-fielding tactics, and on unit 3's sport skills. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: key moves of two games, shown and named; knowledge task: explain a tactic for one sport and one backyard game, then use it in a short game watched by the teacher; conduct: settles a line call with team-talk, S3.12.2 review), 1 respond day. Tools: flying discs, bean bags and a board or a hoop, badminton racquets and shuttles; Curio Class writes the tactic cards | After the mid-year check and unit 3, because tactics in lifetime sports need those sports' skills first; casual games are paired here so both tactic codes are taught in one place | Candidate: a family backyard-game card in the family note | Safety read (striking implements: racquets). Racquet space, no one between a thrower and a target. Low-cost gear named so families can join. Cost: $195 (15 discs, bean bags and a board, 15 racquets and shuttles; cardboard boards on the low-equipment path) |
306: | 5 | 9 | Dance by design. *How do dancers build a dance, and can I design a phrase that says something?* | 12 | g9-dance-design; g9-dance-forms | SHAPE.S1.12.4; SHAPE.S2.12.4 | Cues: "count in, phrase of eight, clear start and end"; form words canon, call and response. Settings: a set sequence learned as a class, then groups design a phrase and join a dance form | Perform a set movement sequence in one dance style and design a short original phrase with a stated idea, a motif and a transition (S1.12.4). Use movement sequences to create or join dance forms: a canon, a call and response, and a social dance (S2.12.4). Picks: a canon and a call and response built from the class's own phrases (no cultural claim), and the Virginia reel, a US longways social dance, danced with teacher-set partners and no gender roles. Opens with retrieval on grade 8's fitness and dance sessions; readiness check on grade 7's dance ideas (canon and contrast) in the last ten minutes of unit 4's respond day. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: perform the set sequence and the group's designed phrase; knowledge task: name the form of a shown dance and join it; conduct: encourages a group member during design, S3.12.3 review), 1 respond day. Product: the group's phrase, drafted, shown for written peer and teacher feedback and revised before the check, inside the unit's days. Tools: music track or a drum; Curio Class writes the dance cards and the Virginia reel's short source line | Dance in grade 9 follows the sport units so the class trusts each other before performing; it comes before unit 6 so the activity sampling there can include dance as a choice | Candidate: a class canon performed for another class | Cultural content: the community is the US and English country dance tradition behind the Virginia reel; partners set by the teacher, no gender roles, no costume. The agent studies a national folklife archive's entry first (For the lead 3). Sacred or ceremonial dances never used. A student who prefers not to perform for others performs for the teacher. Swap allowed. Cost: $0 |
307: | 6 | 9 | My plan to get better. *How do I plan practice to reach a goal I choose, and which activities fit me?* | 13 | g9-skill-practice-plan; g9-exercise-principles; g9-choose-active | SHAPE.S2.12.6; SHAPE.S2.12.7 | Cues: "baseline, plan, practice, retest"; principle words overload, progression, specificity, reversibility. Settings: own chosen skill at stations; sampling days of yoga, aerobics, dance, a sport and an outdoor activity | Set a goal for a skill the student chooses and plan practice to reach it: baseline, drills, number of sessions, a peer check and a retest (S2.12.6; goal-cycle). Apply the exercise principles, overload, progression, specificity and reversibility, to one lifelong activity the student picks from units 1 to 5 (S2.12.7). Sample activities of many kinds (yoga, aerobics, dance, a sport, an outdoor activity) and log privately what each gave: expression, company, health, fit with interests and challenge level, begun toward SHAPE.S4.12.1, SHAPE.S4.12.2, SHAPE.S4.12.3, SHAPE.S4.12.4 and SHAPE.S4.12.5 (owned in grade 10, unit 12); calm-steps used after a hard try. Opens with retrieval on grade 8's goals and obstacles and its reflection, on unit 1's FITT, and on health 9 to 12 units 2 and 15 (helps and barriers; measuring a plan), taught that fall (Cross-subject). Ends: 2 check days (skill task: the retest of the chosen skill against its baseline cue; knowledge task: unseen items, apply each principle to a pictured activity; conduct: a fair peer check of a partner's skill, S3.12.4 review), 1 respond day. Product: the practice plan, drafted on day 3 with written teacher feedback and revised before practice; the activity log is kept private, never scored on what was chosen. Curio Class writes the plan template and the log | Last in grade 9 because goals and principles look back on the year's activities and the sampling needs dance (unit 5) and the sports (units 3, 4) as choices; it hands the log to grade 10's personal plan | Candidate: a retest of unit 1's twelve-minute log | Choice: no activity's value is ranked; logs are private. Emotions: calm steps offered, never a punishment; the teacher page names the school counselor. Yoga taught as stretching and breathing, with no spiritual practice. Cost: $0 (reuses mats and gear) |
308: | 7 | 10 | Train for skill and flexibility. *How do I stretch and train agility and power without getting hurt?* | 11 | g10-stretch-technique; g10-skill-fitness-training | SHAPE.S1.12.7; SHAPE.S1.12.8 | Cues: "dynamic before, static after, hold thirty, no bounce"; "soft landing, quick feet, eyes up". Settings: warm-up routines, then agility, reaction and low plyometric stations | Correct stretching technique: dynamic stretches in the warm-up and static holds for six large muscle groups after training, held about thirty seconds without bouncing, never to pain (S1.12.7). Correct technique when training the skill-related parts: agility ladders and cone drills, reaction drills, low plyometrics (small hops and bounds to a soft landing), balance and coordination work (S1.12.8). Opens with retrieval on unit 1's endurance form, unit 2's strength form and unit 3's sport drills; readiness check on grade 8's fitness sessions and grade 7's skill-related fitness use in the last ten minutes of day 1. Out: partner-assisted stretching. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: stretches for six muscle groups with form, and a short skill-related session with form checked; knowledge task: which stretch goes before and after and why; conduct: keeps the landing zone clear for others, S3.12.2 review), 1 respond day. Tools: tape ladders, cones, mats; Curio Class writes the stretch and drill cue sheets | First in grade 10 because every grade 10 session's warm-up and cool-down uses the stretch technique, and the mixed plan in unit 8 needs skill-related and flexibility training as parts | Candidate: a warm-up routine a group designs and leads | Safety read (plyometrics, mats). Stretch warm muscles only; low heights and small volume; soft surface for landings (Required access). A student with a joint or back concern follows the nurse's guidance. Cost: $15 (tape and cones; reuses mats) |
309: | 8 | 10 | My fitness plan. *Can I build a plan that mixes endurance, strength and flexibility with rest, follow it for weeks, and judge what a tool shows me?* | 15 | g10-mixed-plan; g10-rest; g10-tech | SHAPE.S2.12.8; SHAPE.S2.12.12; SHAPE.S2.12.15 | Cues: FITT for each type; "rest is part of the plan"; "what did the tool show, and can I trust it?". Settings: plan days, then the student's own sessions in class for three weeks, logged | Make and follow a plan that mixes endurance, strength and flexibility training for several weeks: FITT for each type, a weekly schedule, a log, and a change after a mid-way check (S2.12.8). Build rest into the schedule: rest days, sleep and the signs of overtraining (S2.12.12). Examine and use a technology that supports an active life for two weeks of the plan, then judge its accuracy, privacy and what it showed (S2.12.15; the non-screen path in Devices). Opens with retrieval on grade 9 units 1 and 6 (zones, principles) and on health 9 to 12 unit 15's measure-and-revise step (Cross-subject); readiness check on grade 7's rest and grade 8's technology judgment in the last ten minutes of unit 7's respond day. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: one planned session done with form, watched in rotation; knowledge task: the plan, the log and one change with its reason, plus where rest goes and why; the tool judgment in five lines; conduct: shares stations and equipment by the schedule, S3.12.2 review), 1 respond day. Product: the plan drafted on day 3, written teacher feedback, revised on day 4; the log runs through the unit and is scored on the change and its reason, never on results | After unit 7 because the plan needs flexibility and skill-related training as parts; before the mid-year check so the plan runs long enough to change once | Candidate: the plan's log reused unscored at the year-end check | The plan and log are private and never graded on results. No data leaves the student; no personal device required; no product or app named. Sleep talk stays general (no diagnosis). Sensitivity read sources: the federal physical activity guidelines' adolescent pages. Cost: $0 |
310: | 9 | 10 | Games across cultures. *How have games and sport shaped societies, and how can I lead a group through a game that is new to us?* | 11 | g10-games-culture; g10-challenges-leadership | SHAPE.S2.12.14; SHAPE.S3.12.5; SHAPE.S3.12.7; SHAPE.S3.12.8 | Cues: the game's rule card; "plan, explain, watch, adjust" for a leader. Settings: small groups, each led in turn by a student | How games, sports and dance have shaped societies over time, with one case: the revival of the Olympic Games in 1896 and who could compete (S2.12.14); how one activity is valued in different cultures, with one case: association football (soccer) as played and valued in England, where its first common rules were written in 1863, and in Brazil (S3.12.5). Play a game in a no-contact form: sepak takraw, a kick-volley net game from Southeast Asia (played in Malaysia, Thailand and nearby countries), with a light ball and a low net. Reason through challenges alone and in teams during activity (S3.12.7) and judge how well leadership works in different settings, leading a small group through one game segment and evaluating it (S3.12.8). Opens with retrieval on grade 9 unit 2's feedback; readiness check on grade 8's culture row in the last ten minutes of unit 8's respond day. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: sepak takraw key moves in a rally, the unit's baseline cue; knowledge task: the two-culture case, drafted on day 6 with written feedback and revised; conduct and knowledge: leads a segment and evaluates the leadership on a card), 1 respond day | Culture and leadership sit together because teaching a game new to the class is a real leadership challenge; it follows the plan unit so the mid-year check can sample grade 10's S2 and S3 | Candidate: a two-culture case on a game a student's family plays, only with the student's choice | Cultural content: communities named are Southeast Asian (sepak takraw), English and Brazilian football cultures, and the Olympic movement's history. Facts are sourced: the International Olympic Committee's history pages and a national archive's or national football association's history page; the agent studies the Asian Sepaktakraw Federation's or a national sepak takraw body's pages first (For the lead 3). No sacred or ceremonial game. A student may choose a different case. Cost: $20 (light kick balls; a rope as a net) |
311: | 10 | 10 | Outdoors. *How do I move, plan a route and stay safe outdoors, and what should I eat and drink for a day of it?* | 14 | g10-outdoor; g10-outdoor-tactics; g10-safe-habits; g10-food | SHAPE.S1.12.3; SHAPE.S2.12.3; SHAPE.S3.12.6; SHAPE.S2.12.16 | Cues: "map set to the land, thumb on the map"; "pace for the group"; the safety checklist. Settings: an orienteering course on school grounds, a walking or hiking route, cycling where the school has bikes | The key moves of outdoor activities the school can offer: orienteering (setting a map, pacing, a control course), hiking or brisk walking on a route, and cycling or a snow sport where local (S1.12.3). Outdoor tactics: route choice, pacing, reading weather and ground, and risk (S2.12.3), with a route plan. Safe habits: preventing injury, giving space, drinking water, caring for gear, following rules, protecting skin from sun (S3.12.6). Foods and drinks that help or hurt performance, recovery and enjoyment: water, meal timing, and the limits of sports drinks and supplements (S2.12.16), opened as retrieval on health 9 to 12 unit 6's source rating on a supplement question (Cross-subject); PE teaches what to carry for an outdoor day. Opens with retrieval on grade 9 units 1, 3 and 4; readiness check on grade 8's outdoor safety and grade 7's eating and moving well in the last ten minutes of the mid-year respond day. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: an orienteering course of five controls, or the route's key moves; knowledge task: plans a route and an outdoor day's food and water and explains the choices; conduct: follows the safety checklist throughout), 1 respond day. Tools: printed school-ground maps, optional compasses; Curio Class draws the map template and the checklist | After the mid-year check, when the weather turns toward spring; it uses unit 9's group leadership on the route and grade 9's tactics | Candidate: a family walking route card | Safety read (outdoor pursuits). Weather, route, helmets for cycling and supervision ratio set by the school; students stay in groups; trips off site only under the school's trip policy (Required access). Food: no body weight, calorie or diet talk; foods a class commonly sees; allergies respected; supplements named only as limits, never advice. Sun protection and water. Sensitivity read sources: the federal dietary guidelines' pages for teens. Swap allowed for a local outdoor activity. Cost: $10 (map printing; compasses $120 optional) |
312: | 11 | 10 | Safe near water. *What would I do if someone were in trouble in the water, without going in?* | 7 | g10-water | SHAPE.S1.12.9; SHAPE.S2.12.17 | Cues: "reach or throw, don't go"; "buckle, zip, check". Settings: dry land, pairs and small groups | Show water safety on dry land: fasten a life jacket on self and partner, a reach rescue with a pole or towel and a throw rescue to a target with a rope (S1.12.9, dry-land skill task for every school; a survival float and a stroke in water only in a school's own aquatics program). Show understanding of water safety and basic swimming: cold-water shock, currents, hypothermia, calling for help, and what a survival float and a stroke are, shown on the floor (S2.12.17). Readiness check on grade 8's water row in the last ten minutes of unit 10's respond day. Ends: 1 check day (skill task: life jacket and a throw rescue to a target; knowledge task: what to do in a pictured emergency at open water; conduct: checks a partner's buckle, S3.12.3 review), 1 respond day. Tools: life jackets, a throw rope or bag, a pole | Before summer, when teenagers are near open water; short because its two codes are taught on dry land in one setting | Candidate: a family water-safety card | Water: no student is made to enter water; the course page says in-water tasks are the school's own program with a lifeguard. A student who fears water or has lost someone to drowning may take a helper role; the teacher page says so. Sources: CDC's "Preventing Drowning" page (opened 2026-11-25). Safety read (water). Required access: real life jackets, borrowed or bought. Cost: $140 (4 life jackets, a throw rope) |
