325: | 2 | Level 1 | Body, rhythm and rehearsal. *How do dancers make a phrase clear, alive and repeatable, and how do they practice to get there?* | 38 | da-g9-elements; da-g9-technique | DA:Pr4.1.Ia; DA:Pr4.1.Ib; DA:Pr4.1.Ic; DA:Pr5.1.Ia; DA:Pr5.1.Ib; DA:Pr5.1.Ic | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); responding through the works watched (review); connecting through the physical education pair named below. Works: Michelle Dorrance and Dorrance Dance, SOUNDspace (United States, 2013; tap, the dancers' feet as the music; the company's own page), described and linked: syncopation and accents; Germaine Acogny, À un endroit du début (Senegal, 2015, a solo; born 1944, founder of the École des Sables), described and linked to the school's or the presenting theater's page: energy carried through the whole body | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: the bridge's elements, accent and timing, warm-up and phrase recall. DA:Pr4.1.Ia: build partner and group sections with low balances (counterbalance, shared weight at floor level) at contrasting levels; the id allows lifts or balances, and balances meet it (no lifts, APPROACH "Safe use"), with a solo version of every balance. DA:Pr4.1.Ib: use syncopation and accents at varied tempos, take cues from the accompaniment, and link breath to phrasing. DA:Pr4.1.Ic: carry energy and dynamics through the whole body so phrases show variety. DA:Pr5.1.Ia: use technical skill to retain and perform a 32-count Curio Class phrase. DA:Pr5.1.Ib: plan healthy habits for dance and daily life (warm-up, cool-down, rest, water, sleep) for an invented dancer, with food through that invented dancer, and say how the plan supports the dancer's goals. DA:Pr5.1.Ic: plan a rehearsal with peers, analyze a run of the phrase with an observer's sheet or the school's recording, and justify the practice choices. Words: syncopation, accent, balance, contrast, retain, rehearsal plan. Physical education boundary: grade 9 unit 5 teaches set sequences and dance forms as movement skill; this unit teaches performance quality and rehearsal, and names that unit in its connections line. Showing: the partner and group phrase for another class. Evidence, id by id. Mid-unit assessment (a showing day, about day 17 of the unit): DA:Pr4.1.Ib, a look-for: accents and syncopation on the counts, the tempo change and the music cue taken; DA:Pr4.1.Ic, a look-for: energy shown through the whole body with two contrasting dynamics. End-of-unit assessment (a showing day, then a kept-piece day): DA:Pr4.1.Ia, a look-for: a low balance at a contrasting level held with the partner or the group (or its solo version); DA:Pr5.1.Ia, a look-for: the 32-count phrase performed from memory with its spacing and timing; DA:Pr5.1.Ib, a kept piece: the healthy-habits plan for the invented dancer and how it supports the dancer's goals, drafted, given written feedback and revised; DA:Pr5.1.Ic, a kept piece: the group's rehearsal plan, the observer's notes and a written justification of two practice choices. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: purposeful space and aerial work (Level 2), lifts (never). Tools: step cards, the balance card with its solo version, a rehearsal plan sheet, an observer's sheet, a beat keeper or its printed count card, Curio Class's own. Free path: claps or a drum for the beat. Short path: 18 days | First owning unit because every later Level 1 unit dances phrases that need clear elements and a rehearsal routine; the rehearsal-plan model starts here, and unit 5's concert uses it. Longest with unit 4: six ids, three of them performed and scored live | Candidate: a third work with a strong rhythm from Asia, described and linked | Hazards and controls: balances low to the floor, by agreement, with a solo version always offered; no lifts; partner contact by agreement and never required; tap is watched, not done in hard shoes on the class floor (taps damage floors and ankles without training); the warm-up before every dancing day. Food: the plan is for an invented dancer; no task asks about a student's own diet, weight or body (APPROACH "Sensitive ground"). Tap's history: the sensitivity agent reads the company's own page and a dance history archive's page on tap's African American and Irish roots first; Acogny: the École des Sables' own published words first. Health's steps if a student tells something. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
326: | 3 | Level 1 | Making a dance. *How does a source become a dance, and how does a dance's shape serve what it is for?* | 34 | da-g9-ideas; da-g9-form | DA:Cr1.1.Ia; DA:Cr1.1.Ib; DA:Cr2.1.Ia; DA:Cr2.1.Ib | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); responding through the two works (review). Works: Trisha Brown, Accumulation (United States, 1971; Brown 1936 to 2017; the Trisha Brown Dance Company's page), described and linked: one gesture added at a time, a structure that serves the idea; Rennie Harris, Rome and Jewels (United States, 2000, hip hop dance theater from Romeo and Juliet; the company's page), described and linked: how a stimulus shaped a piece; Shen Wei, Folding (2000; born in China, works in the United States; the company's page), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: the bridge's inspiration list, movement challenge, start, middle and finish, and the four tools. DA:Cr1.1.Ia: try many sources of movement (a text, an image, a sound, an object, a place) for one piece and analyze in the process log how each shaped it. DA:Cr1.1.Ib: test dance elements to find the student's own movement strengths and choose demanding moves for an original piece. DA:Cr2.1.Ia: make a dance with others whose structure (theme and variation, ABA, accumulation, narrative) serves its purpose, and explain how. DA:Cr2.1.Ib: write an artist's statement for the dance and discuss how its structure and elements support it. Words: stimulus, strengths, structure, purpose, artist's statement, accumulation. Showing: the small-group dances, with their statements read aloud, for the class. Evidence, id by id. Mid-unit assessment: DA:Cr1.1.Ia, a kept piece: the log entry for three sources tried and an analysis of how each shaped the piece; DA:Cr1.1.Ib, a kept piece with a look-for: the strengths test in the log and two demanding moves chosen, shown at the teacher's spot. End-of-unit assessment (a showing day, then the statement): DA:Cr2.1.Ia, a look-for on the showing: the structure named and visible, and a spoken line on how it serves the purpose; DA:Cr2.1.Ib, a kept piece: the artist's statement on structure and elements, drafted, given written feedback after the critique and revised. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: blending established movement and styles (Level 2). Tools: source cards, the strengths test sheet, structure cards, the statement frame, Curio Class's own. Free path: sources from the room, a library book or a public domain poem. Short path: 16 days | After unit 2 because a dance is made from clear elements and rehearsed phrases; before unit 4 because a dance must exist to be reworked and recorded. The artist-statement model starts here. The mid-year check follows it | Candidate: a choreographer from Africa or Latin America whose source is a place, described and linked | Hazards and controls: as unit 2; group making on a marked floor with zones so groups do not collide. Hip hop's Philadelphia roots: the sensitivity agent reads the company's own published words first. Sources from a student's own life are invited, never required, with a public or invented source marked the same. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
327: | 4 | Level 1 | Seeing patterns, changing a dance. *How do patterns carry a dance's purpose in its culture, and how does a choreographer know a change made it better?* | 38 | da-g9-revise; da-g9-respond | DA:Cr3.1.Ia; DA:Cr3.1.Ib; DA:Re7.1.Ia; DA:Re7.1.Ib; DA:Re8.1.Ia; DA:Re9.1.Ia | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re7, Re8, Re9). Works: Anne Teresa De Keersmaeker, Rosas danst Rosas (Belgium, 1983; the Rosas company's page), described and linked: repeated patterns that serve a purpose; Maria Tallchief in George Balanchine's The Firebird (New York City Ballet, 1949; Tallchief, a citizen of the Osage Nation, 1925 to 2013), described and linked to the company's or a museum's page; Gregory Maqoma, Exit/Exist (South Africa, 2012, a solo on the life of the Xhosa chief Maqoma; the artist's page), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: the bridge's try-a-change routine, motif symbols, describe and interpret, and the criteria card. DA:Re7.1.Ia: analyze how repeated movement patterns and the dancers' relationships serve a dance's purpose. DA:Re7.1.Ib: analyze how elements across at least two styles and traditions (the three works) carry intent in their cultural setting. DA:Re8.1.Ia: compare two dances, discussing intent and expression and how elements and setting strengthen the meaning. DA:Re9.1.Ia: analyze a dance's expression and discuss what was learned, using evaluation criteria and dance terms. DA:Cr3.1.Ia: rework the devices and structure of the unit 3 dance to make its purpose clearer and evaluate in the log what each revision did. DA:Cr3.1.Ib: compare two established notation methods (motif notation and Labanotation) by recording one 8-count section in each. Words: pattern, relationship, cultural context, criteria, revision, notation. Showing: the revised dance beside its first version, for the class. Evidence, id by id. Mid-unit assessment (a written sitting on an unseen Curio Class dance and a written description of a second): DA:Re7.1.Ia, a written item: a repeated pattern and the purpose it serves; DA:Re7.1.Ib, a written item: how an element carries intent in each dance's stated cultural setting. End-of-unit assessment: DA:Re8.1.Ia, a kept piece: a comparison of two studied dances, drafted, given written feedback and revised; DA:Re9.1.Ia, a written item in the end sitting: the unseen dance's expression analyzed with three criteria and dance terms; DA:Cr3.1.Ia, a look-for on the showing and the log: two revisions to devices or structure and an evaluation of each; DA:Cr3.1.Ib, a kept piece: one section recorded in both methods with two differences named. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: weighing societal values (Level 2), a whole dance notated (Level 3). Tools: work cards, the notation sheets for both methods, the criteria card, the change log, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencil and printed sheets. Short path: 19 days | After unit 3 because the revision reworks unit 3's dance and the analysis uses its structure words; before unit 5 because the concert shows the revised dance and research asks for the analysis habits | Candidate: a dance from Asia for the comparison, described and linked | Tallchief: the sensitivity agent reads the Osage Nation's own published words and a museum's page on her first; the unit names her as an Osage ballerina in her own and her nation's words, and no student is asked about their own heritage. Maqoma's solo tells of a chief who fought colonial rule: the agent reads the artist's own published words first; the unit tells it plainly from them, with no role play. Hazards and controls: as unit 2. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
328: | 5 | Level 1 | The concert and the question. *How do research and design shape a dance for an audience, and why do dances from different times differ?* | 34 | da-g9-stage; da-g9-connect | DA:Pr6.1.Ia; DA:Pr6.1.Ib; DA:Cn10.1.Ia; DA:Cn10.1.Ib; DA:Cn11.1.Ia | Performing (Pr6); connecting (Cn10, Cn11); creating as the team makes its answer dance. Works: Katherine Dunham, L'Ag'Ya (1938, a stage work from her research on a martial dance of Martinique; Dunham 1909 to 2006, an anthropologist who studied dance in Martinique, Haiti and Jamaica; the Library of Congress's Katherine Dunham collection pages), described and linked; Frankie Manning (1914 to 2009) and the Lindy Hop at Harlem's Savoy Ballroom, from the 1930s, described and linked to a dance history archive's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: the bridge's stage terms and etiquette, the research notes and the like-me line. DA:Cn10.1.Ia: analyze with evidence a dance's ideas and how the choreographer's view (Dunham's research) shapes the student's reading. DA:Cn10.1.Ib: research a dance question with a team (for example, how did a social dance travel between two places) and make a dance that answers it, then compare the team's process with a classmate team's. DA:Cn11.1.Ia: compare dances from two chosen styles or periods (the Lindy Hop and L'Ag'Ya, or one of them beside a unit 3 work) and explain why they differ through their people's views. DA:Pr6.1.Ia: show leadership and etiquette in rehearsal, apply notes, and evaluate documented rehearsal methods with proper terms. DA:Pr6.1.Ib: evaluate production design options (light, space, costume as plain color, sound) and carry out the ones that best serve the dance's purpose. Words: research question, evidence, period, production design, etiquette, notes. Showing: the Level 1 concert for families (unit 4's revised dance and the team answer dances), with the family's yes, or the student's own at 18 or over, for any showing outside class. Evidence, id by id. Mid-unit assessment: DA:Cn10.1.Ia, a written item in a sitting: the ideas of a described dance and how its maker's view shapes the reading, with evidence; DA:Cn11.1.Ia, a kept piece: the two-period comparison with reasons, drafted, given written feedback and revised. End-of-unit assessment (the concert, then the process record): DA:Cn10.1.Ib, a look-for on the answer dance and a kept piece: the team's question, its research notes and the process comparison; DA:Pr6.1.Ia, a look-for in the dress rehearsal and a kept piece: notes applied and an evaluation of two documented rehearsal methods in proper terms; DA:Pr6.1.Ib, a kept piece with a look-for: two design options judged and the chosen one carried out in the concert. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a whole concert planned by the class (Level 2). Tools: research note cards, the production design sheet, the rehearsal evaluation sheet, a concert program frame, Curio Class's own. Free path: house lights and a doorway, plain clothes in one color. Short path: 17 days | Last in Level 1 because the concert shows the revised dance and the year's making, and research needs the analysis habits of unit 4; the production-plan model starts here. Its concert in weeks 31 and 32 is its own end-of-unit assessment, before the year-end sitting | Candidate: a third period's dance from Asia for the comparison, described and linked | Dunham studied Vodou ritual dance in Haiti: the unit names it and studies it by reading and watching only; no student dances or imitates it (STANDARD.md 5 leave-out line); the sensitivity agent reads the Library of Congress's Dunham pages first. The Lindy Hop: the Savoy was a place where Black and white dancers met in a segregated city; the agent reads an archive's page and Manning's own published words first. A research question from a student's own family is optional, with a public question marked the same. Production hazards and controls: no student on a ladder, cables taped, no haze, no strobe. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
329: | 6 | Level 2 | Back on the floor (review and placement). *What does a Level 1 dancer bring, and where does each of us start?* | 6 |  | None | Performing, creating and responding as review of Level 1. Works: the class's own Level 1 portfolio pieces; one Level 1 work revisited (Rosas danst Rosas, unit 4) | The Level 2 review unit (STANDARD.md 3.2): at most 6 days, counted inside the standards, that doubles as placement. Review: the warm-up, balances, syncopation, the artist's statement, motif notation, the criteria card and the research notes, each at Level 1 depth. Placement: a student new to the school shows a phrase, a short statement and a written analysis scored against the Level 1 year-end check and unit 5's guide. Words: review, placement, goal. Showing: each student's short Level 1 phrase, re-danced, for the class. Evidence: the readiness record sorts each student met, partly or not yet on each thread; placement marks go to the counselor's note. No Level 2 id is scored here. Out: every Level 2 id (units 7 to 10). Tools: the review cards, the placement sheet, Curio Class's own. Short path: 3 days | First in Level 2 because a student may arrive from Level 1 the year before, after a gap, or new to the school; the review is the readiness check for every Level 2 row and its results shape units 7 and 8 | Candidate: a self-check form a student new to the school can fill in before day 1 | Hazards and controls: as unit 1; a student new to dance placed by mistake moves to Level 1 without a mark against them. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
330: | 7 | Level 2 | Space, time and the body's care. *How do dancers use space and time on purpose, and how do they keep a body able to do it?* | 40 | da-g10-elements; da-g10-technique | DA:Pr4.1.IIa; DA:Pr4.1.IIb; DA:Pr4.1.IIc; DA:Pr5.1.IIa; DA:Pr5.1.IIb; DA:Pr5.1.IIc | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); responding through the works (review). Works: Merce Cunningham, Beach Birds (United States, 1991; Cunningham 1919 to 2009; the Merce Cunningham Trust's page), described and linked: time not set to the music, an inner rhythm; Rodrigo Pederneiras and Grupo Corpo, Benguelê (Brazil, 1998; the company's page), described and linked: ground and aerial sequences in a large group | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: Level 1's balances, syncopation and rehearsal plan (unit 2). DA:Pr4.1.IIa: dance alone and with others, using space on purpose in complex ground sequences (on the floor: rolls on the side, slides, level changes) and aerial sequences (jumps and leaps from the floor, landings taught first). DA:Pr4.1.IIb: perform with time used unpredictably, phrase from an inner rhythm, and respond spontaneously to a change in the room or the music. DA:Pr4.1.IIc: start each phrase with energy, vary dynamics along it and pass smoothly into the next. DA:Pr5.1.IIa: dance with others through demanding sequences of space, rhythm and dynamics to meet set goals. DA:Pr5.1.IIb: apply anatomy (the joints and muscles a move uses, alignment, safe landing) and healthy habits to technical skills, and build an eating plan for an invented dancer in a week of rehearsals (the id's personal nutrition plan is met through the invented dancer, by Cory's rule, For the lead 3). DA:Pr5.1.IIc: plan solo and group rehearsals for technique and artistry and reflect on what was achieved. Words: ground, aerial, inner rhythm, transition, anatomy, goal. Showing: the group sequence for another class. Evidence, id by id. Mid-unit assessment (a showing day): DA:Pr4.1.IIa, a look-for: a ground and an aerial passage with space used on purpose and safe landings; DA:Pr4.1.IIb, a look-for: an unpredictable timing choice and a spontaneous response to the teacher's cue. End-of-unit assessment (a showing day, then a kept-piece day): DA:Pr4.1.IIc, a look-for: phrases started with energy and linked without a stop; DA:Pr5.1.IIa, a look-for: the demanding group sequence meeting its set goals in space, rhythm and dynamics; DA:Pr5.1.IIb, a kept piece: an anatomy note on two moves and the invented dancer's eating plan, drafted, given written feedback and revised; DA:Pr5.1.IIc, a kept piece: the solo and group rehearsal plans and a reflection on what they achieved. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: the fullest spatial range toward an audience (Level 3). Tools: step cards for landings and floor work, an anatomy card, the rehearsal plan sheets, Curio Class's own. Free path: claps, a drum, the voice. Short path: 20 days | First owning unit of Level 2 because unit 8's blended styles and unit 10's concert need purposeful space and planned rehearsals; the same slot as unit 2, one level harder | Candidate: a work from Asia with ground work, described and linked | Hazards and controls: aerial work is jumps and leaps from the floor only, landings taught before any jump, no jumping from a raised surface; floor work on a clean floor, knees and elbows protected by technique (rolling on the side, never on the spine or the head); no inverted moves; no lifts. Food: the eating plan is for an invented dancer; no task asks about a student's own diet, weight or body; health's steps if a student tells something. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
331: | 8 | Level 2 | Blending styles. *How do choreographers blend their own movement with established styles, and what makes a dance's choices their own aesthetic?* | 34 | da-g10-ideas; da-g10-form | DA:Cr1.1.IIa; DA:Cr1.1.IIb; DA:Cr2.1.IIa; DA:Cr2.1.IIb | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); responding as the choreographers are compared. Works: Twyla Tharp, Push Comes to Shove (United States, 1976, ballet blended with popular dance; the artist's page), described and linked; Kyle Abraham, Pavement (United States, 2012, hip hop and modern dance; the company's page), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: Level 1's sources, strengths test and artist's statement (unit 3). DA:Cr1.1.IIa: combine gathered source material to choreograph a dance from invented and established movement. DA:Cr1.1.IIb: blend the student's movement strengths with more than one style into a purposeful dance, and compare the approach with that of two known choreographers (Tharp and Abraham). DA:Cr2.1.IIa: design original dances alone and with others, and analyze how structure and composition serve the purpose. DA:Cr2.1.IIb: write a statement of the student's aesthetic for a dance and choose movements that support it. Words: invented, established, blend, composition, aesthetic, compare. Showing: a solo or duet study and a group dance, with the aesthetic statements, for the class. Evidence, id by id. Mid-unit assessment: DA:Cr1.1.IIa, a look-for on the solo or duet study: invented and established movement both present and joined; DA:Cr1.1.IIb, a kept piece: the comparison of the student's approach with Tharp's and Abraham's. End-of-unit assessment (a showing day, then the statement): DA:Cr2.1.IIa, a look-for and a spoken line: the group dance's structure and composition and how they serve its purpose; DA:Cr2.1.IIb, a kept piece: the aesthetic statement and the movements chosen to support it, drafted, given written feedback after the critique and revised. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: risk and a personal voice (Level 3). Tools: the style menu card, the composition card, the aesthetic statement frame, Curio Class's own. Free path: as unit 3. Short path: 17 days | After unit 7 because blended styles need its purposeful space and timing; before unit 9 because the dance made here is the one refined and recorded there. The mid-year check follows it | Candidate: a choreographer from Asia or Africa who blends styles, described and linked | Established movement from a community's dance is learned only where its own teaching says it is open to be taught; no costume or imitation of a people (the row's teacher note). Hazards and controls: as unit 7. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
332: | 9 | Level 2 | One dance, three times. *How do the values of a society shape how a dance is made, seen and judged?* | 40 | da-g10-revise; da-g10-respond | DA:Cr3.1.IIa; DA:Cr3.1.IIb; DA:Re7.1.IIa; DA:Re7.1.IIb; DA:Re8.1.IIa; DA:Re9.1.IIa | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re7, Re8, Re9). Works: The Rite of Spring in three versions: Vaslav Nijinsky's 1913 Paris premiere (known from the 1987 reconstruction by Millicent Hodson and Kenneth Archer), Pina Bausch's 1975 version (Germany, the Pina Bausch Foundation's page), and the 2021 staging of Bausch's version with dancers from 14 African countries, made with the École des Sables (Senegal), each described and linked; Ahn Eun-me, Dancing Grandmothers (Korea, 2011; the company's page), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: Level 1's notation methods, change log and criteria card (unit 4). DA:Re7.1.IIa: analyze works, giving examples of repeated patterns that build form and meaning. DA:Re7.1.IIb: compare movement patterns across styles and traditions and explain how the differences shape what a dance says in its culture. DA:Re8.1.IIa: analyze how elements, the execution of dance principles and the setting shape expression. DA:Re9.1.IIa: compare several dances with criteria to critique their expression, weighing the values and views of their societies (why the 1913 audience rioted; what Ahn's dancers say about age). DA:Cr3.1.IIa: refine unit 8's dance against criteria and feedback, and evaluate the revisions. DA:Cr3.1.IIb: plan a strategy to record the whole dance with notation or media. Words: form, meaning, setting, values, refine, recording plan. Showing: the refined dance for another class. Evidence, id by id. Mid-unit assessment (a written sitting): DA:Re7.1.IIa, a written item on an unseen Curio Class dance: two repeated patterns and the form and meaning they build; DA:Re8.1.IIa, a written item on a described dance: how elements, execution and setting shape its expression. End-of-unit assessment: DA:Re7.1.IIb, a kept piece: a comparison of patterns in two of the four works and what the differences say in each culture; DA:Re9.1.IIa, a kept piece: a critique of three dances with criteria, weighing each society's values, drafted, given written feedback and revised; DA:Cr3.1.IIa, a look-for on the showing and the log: revisions made against named criteria and feedback, each evaluated; DA:Cr3.1.IIb, a kept piece: the recording plan for the whole dance. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a complete dance notated (Level 3). Tools: work cards for each version, the criteria card, the notation sheets, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencil and sheets. Short path: 20 days | After unit 8 because the refinement reworks its dance; before unit 10 because the concert shows the refined dance and the research there needs this comparison habit | Candidate: a fourth dance from the Americas, described and linked | The Rite of Spring stages an invented sacrificial rite: it is a stage fiction, studied by watching and reading only, with no role play of the sacrifice (STANDARD.md 5 leave-out line). The 2021 cast: the sensitivity agent reads the École des Sables' and the foundation's own published words first. Dancing Grandmothers: the agent reads the company's own words first; a tie to a student's own grandparents is optional, with an invented or public option marked the same. Hazards and controls: as unit 7. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
333: | 10 | Level 2 | Light, motion and the whole concert. *What can a dance share with another subject, and how does a team stage a whole concert?* | 36 | da-g10-stage; da-g10-connect | DA:Pr6.1.IIa; DA:Pr6.1.IIb; DA:Cn10.1.IIa; DA:Cn10.1.IIb; DA:Cn11.1.IIa | Performing (Pr6); connecting (Cn10, Cn11); creating as teams solve movement problems. Works: Loie Fuller (United States and France, 1862 to 1928), the serpentine dance and her patented stage lighting and costume designs, shown from a public domain film of a serpentine dance in the Library of Congress's collections and described from an archive's page; Alwin Nikolais, Tensile Involvement (United States, 1955; the Nikolais/Louis Foundation's page), described and linked: light, elastic bands and motion | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: Level 1's production plan, etiquette and research notes (unit 5). DA:Cn10.1.IIa: analyze a dance that links to another subject (Fuller's light and fabric with physics, Nikolais's design with visual art), research its setting and share the insight. DA:Cn10.1.IIb: research a topic, solve movement problems on it with others, perform the choreography and discuss what carried over between dance and the topic. DA:Cn11.1.IIa: study dances from many styles and eras (Fuller, Nikolais and the year's works) and link their technique and criteria to their people's views. DA:Pr6.1.IIa: model leadership and etiquette in rehearsal, strengthen projection, apply notes and evaluate the student's own documented process. DA:Pr6.1.IIb: work with a team to stage a whole concert in a theater or another space, from a production plan that covers the production's needs. Words: projection, production plan, concert, transfer, era, insight. Showing: the Level 2 concert for families, planned and run by the class teams, with the family's yes, or the student's own at 18 or over. Evidence, id by id. Mid-unit assessment: DA:Cn10.1.IIa, a kept piece: the linked dance's setting researched and the insight written; DA:Cn11.1.IIa, a written item in a sitting: two dances of different eras, their technique and criteria linked to their people's views. End-of-unit assessment (the concert, then the process record): DA:Cn10.1.IIb, a look-for on the performed piece and a kept piece: what carried over between dance and the topic; DA:Pr6.1.IIa, a look-for in the dress rehearsal and a kept piece: projection, notes applied and an evaluation of the student's own process log; DA:Pr6.1.IIb, a kept piece with a look-for: the team's production plan and the concert staged from it. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: concerts in more than one place (Level 3). Tools: the production plan, research cards, the process log, Curio Class's own. Free path: house lights, a flashlight, fabric from home or the school. Short path: 16 days | Last in Level 2 because the concert shows the year's work and needs the planning habits of Level 1's unit 5 and the comparisons of unit 9. Its concert in weeks 31 and 32 is its own end-of-unit assessment | Candidate: a dance-and-science link from outside the United States and Europe, described and linked | Production hazards and controls: no student on a ladder or a raised edge, cables taped, no hot lamp touched, no haze, no strobe or flashing light; long fabric is never wrapped around a neck. Fuller's films: early films may hold other material; the teacher shows only the dance. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
334: | 11 | Level 3 | The Advanced dancer (review and placement). *What do I bring to Advanced work, and what will I risk this year?* | 6 |  | None | Performing, creating and responding as review of Level 2. Works: the class's own Level 2 portfolio pieces; Beach Birds (unit 7) revisited | The Level 3 review unit (STANDARD.md 3.2): at most 6 days, counted inside the standards, that doubles as placement. Review: ground and aerial work, the aesthetic statement, the recording plan, the critique with societal values and the production plan, each at Level 2 depth. Placement: a student new to the school shows a sequence, an aesthetic statement and a written critique scored against the Level 2 year-end check and unit 10's guide. Words: review, placement, risk. Showing: each student's Level 2 phrase, re-danced, for the class. Evidence: the readiness record sorts each student met, partly or not yet on each thread; no Level 3 id is scored here. Out: every Level 3 id (units 12 to 15). Tools: the review cards, the placement sheet, Curio Class's own. Short path: 3 days | First in Level 3 for the same reason as unit 6; its results shape units 12 and 13 | Candidate: the self-check form of unit 6 at Level 2 depth | Hazards and controls: as unit 7. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
335: | 12 | Level 3 | Body, mind and the audience. *How does a dancer reach an audience with the full range of space, rhythm and dynamics, and lead others to it?* | 40 | da-g11-elements; da-g11-technique | DA:Pr4.1.IIIa; DA:Pr4.1.IIIb; DA:Pr4.1.IIIc; DA:Pr5.1.IIIa; DA:Pr5.1.IIIb; DA:Pr5.1.IIIc | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); responding through the works (review). Works: Lin Hwai-min and Cloud Gate Dance Theatre of Taiwan, Moon Water (Taiwan, 1998, built on tai chi daoyin; the company's page), described and linked: body-mind principles; Ronald K. Brown, Grace (United States, 1999, West African and contemporary dance; the company's page), described and linked: layered rhythms and dynamics toward an audience | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: Level 2's ground and aerial work, transitions and rehearsal plans (unit 7). DA:Pr4.1.IIIa: use the fullest range of space, shifting focus inward and outward and relating to the audience. DA:Pr4.1.IIIb: shape timing for interest, show complex phrasing with or without music, and layer several rhythms. DA:Pr4.1.IIIc: adjust dynamics across a broad range to express an intent and project it to the audience. DA:Pr5.1.IIIa: apply body-mind principles (breath, alignment, the center, release) in complex solo, partner or group work, evaluate the student's own technique and discuss it. DA:Pr5.1.IIIb: research safe dancer practices from a free source (a dance medicine association's or a health agency's page, as of its date), change one of the student's own practices (warm-up, rest, landing, cross-training) from the findings and discuss the effect. DA:Pr5.1.IIIc: lead a rehearsal that attends to technical detail and expression, with varied strategies. Words: focus, layered, project, body-mind, safe practice, lead. Showing: the solo, duet or group piece for another class. Evidence, id by id. Mid-unit assessment (a showing day): DA:Pr4.1.IIIa, a look-for: the full spatial range with focus shifted inward and outward; DA:Pr4.1.IIIb, a look-for: two layered rhythms and a timing choice that holds interest. End-of-unit assessment (a showing day, a led rehearsal day, then a kept piece): DA:Pr4.1.IIIc, a look-for: a broad dynamic range projected to the audience with a stated intent; DA:Pr5.1.IIIa, a look-for and a kept piece: the body-mind principles applied in the piece, with the student's written self-evaluation; DA:Pr5.1.IIIb, a kept piece: the research note on safe practice, the one practice changed and its effect, drafted, given written feedback and revised; DA:Pr5.1.IIIc, a look-for: the student-led rehearsal, its notes on detail and expression and two strategies used. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: nothing further at this level; Level 4 uses these ids again on new work. Tools: the body-mind card, the safe practice research sheet, the led rehearsal sheet, Curio Class's own. Free path: as unit 7. Short path: 20 days | First owning unit of Level 3 because unit 13's risk-taking and unit 15's concerts need the full range and student-led rehearsal; the same slot as units 2 and 7 | Candidate: a European or Latin American work with layered rhythm, described and linked | Moon Water uses tai chi as movement training, not religious practice; the sensitivity agent reads the company's own words first. Grace is set partly to spiritual music: watched, not danced to as worship; the agent reads the company's own words. Safe practice: a student changes their own warm-up, rest or landing by choice; no task asks about their diet, weight or body; health's steps if a student tells something. Hazards and controls: as unit 7; a student-led rehearsal follows the class safety rules, and the teacher keeps the stop cue. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
336: | 13 | Level 3 | A voice of one's own. *How does a choreographer take risks to find a personal voice, and how does a dance carry a viewpoint?* | 34 | da-g11-ideas; da-g11-form | DA:Cr1.1.IIIa; DA:Cr1.1.IIIb; DA:Cr2.1.IIIa; DA:Cr2.1.IIIb | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); responding and connecting through the works (review). Works: Pina Bausch, Café Müller (Germany, 1978; the Pina Bausch Foundation's page), described and linked: risk and an unusual structure; Dada Masilo, Swan Lake (South Africa, 2010, ballet blended with Tswana dance; the unit plan records the maker's dates from her company's page), described and linked: a personal and cultural viewpoint | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: Level 2's blending, composition and aesthetic statement (unit 8). DA:Cr1.1.IIIa: blend source material and take risks to find a personal voice that carries a stated intent. DA:Cr1.1.IIIb: push past the student's usual movement habits for surprising solutions and explain in the log why they strengthened the intent. DA:Cr2.1.IIIa: choreograph original dances fluently in a personal voice, justifying the choices that sharpen the purpose. DA:Cr2.1.IIIb: write a statement of the student's own cultural and artistic viewpoint (or an invented choreographer's, marked the same). Words: risk, voice, intent, habit, viewpoint, justify. Showing: the risk-taking pieces, with the viewpoint statements, for the class. Evidence, id by id. Mid-unit assessment: DA:Cr1.1.IIIa, a look-for on a short study: a stated intent carried by a risk the student names; DA:Cr1.1.IIIb, a kept piece: the log entry naming two habits broken and why each new solution strengthened the intent. End-of-unit assessment (a showing day, then the statement): DA:Cr2.1.IIIa, a look-for and a spoken justification: an original piece in a personal voice with two choices defended; DA:Cr2.1.IIIb, a kept piece: the viewpoint statement, drafted, given written feedback after the critique and revised. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a self-directed series (Level 4). Tools: the habit-breaking card, the viewpoint statement frame, Curio Class's own. Free path: as unit 3. Short path: 17 days | After unit 12 because risk-taking needs the full range and body-mind control; before unit 14 because the dance made here is refined, defended and notated there. The mid-year check follows it | Candidate: a living choreographer from Asia or the Pacific with a personal voice, described and linked | A student names only a heritage they choose to name; no one is asked to represent a group (the row's teacher note); the viewpoint statement may be an invented choreographer's. Masilo's Swan Lake tells a story of a young man who loves another man: the sensitivity agent reads the company's own words first, and the unit tells the work's own story plainly from them. Café Müller: dancers move with eyes closed among chairs; the class never copies that (the chairs and closed eyes are watched, not danced). Hazards and controls: as unit 7; risk is a choreographic choice, never a physical one (no falls, no eyes closed while moving). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
337: | 14 | Level 3 | Beauty, belief and taste. *How do dances carry ideas of beauty and belief, and how do I defend my own taste?* | 40 | da-g11-revise; da-g11-respond | DA:Cr3.1.IIIa; DA:Cr3.1.IIIb; DA:Re7.1.IIIa; DA:Re7.1.IIIb; DA:Re8.1.IIIa; DA:Re9.1.IIIa | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re7, Re8, Re9). Works: Dance Theatre of Harlem, Creole Giselle (United States, 1984, Arthur Mitchell's company, the ballet set among free Creoles of color in Louisiana; the company's page), described and linked; Kazuo Ohno, Admiring La Argentina (Japan, 1977, butoh; Ohno 1906 to 2010; the Kazuo Ohno Dance Studio's page), described and linked; Santee Smith, Kaha:wi (Canada, 2004; Kanien'kehá:ka (Mohawk) choreographer of Six Nations of the Grand River; Kaha:wi Dance Theatre's page), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: Level 2's criteria, critique with societal values and recording plan (unit 9). DA:Re7.1.IIIa: analyze dances in varied genres (ballet, butoh, contemporary Indigenous dance), explaining how repeated patterns build well-structured choreography. DA:Re7.1.IIIb: explain how dance carries ideals of beauty and cultural beliefs across styles and traditions. DA:Re8.1.IIIa: interpret how elements, principles and context shape expression across styles and traditions. DA:Re9.1.IIIa: name the student's own artistic tastes to critique dance, weighing values, and defend the views with peers. DA:Cr3.1.IIIa: refine unit 13's dance through reflection and feedback, and document and defend each revision. DA:Cr3.1.IIIb: record the complete dance with an established notation method (motif notation, or Labanotation for a short dance). Words: genre, ideal, belief, interpret, taste, defend. Showing: the refined, notated dance for another class. Evidence, id by id. Mid-unit assessment (a written sitting): DA:Re7.1.IIIa, a written item on an unseen Curio Class dance: how its repeated patterns build its structure; DA:Re8.1.IIIa, a written item on a described dance from a tradition not studied: how elements, principles and context shape its expression. End-of-unit assessment (a peer discussion day, then the record): DA:Re7.1.IIIb, a kept piece: how two of the three works carry beauty ideals and beliefs, drafted, given written feedback and revised; DA:Re9.1.IIIa, a spoken item in the peer discussion and a kept piece: the student's tastes named and defended against a peer's; DA:Cr3.1.IIIa, a kept piece with a look-for: the revision record, every change documented and defended; DA:Cr3.1.IIIb, a kept piece: the complete dance in the chosen notation. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: nothing further at this level; Level 4's critique set uses these ids again. Tools: work cards, the notation sheets, the discussion card, the revision record, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencil and sheets. Short path: 20 days | After unit 13 because the record refines and notates its dance; before unit 15 because the concerts show it and the growth review draws on this record | Candidate: a fourth genre from Africa or South Asia for the beauty ideals, described and linked | Beauty ideals: the unit studies the ideals in the works and their eras (ballet's thin ideal, butoh's rejection of it); it never discusses students' bodies, weight or diet; health's steps if a student tells something. Kaha:wi draws on Haudenosaunee story: the sensitivity agent reads Santee Smith's own published words and the company's page first; it is watched, never danced or imitated (STANDARD.md 5 leave-out line). Creole Giselle: the agent reads the company's own words on the setting and its history first. Hazards and controls: as unit 7. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
338: | 15 | Level 3 | Two stages and a life in dance. *How does a dance change from one place to another, and where could dance take me?* | 36 | da-g11-stage; da-g11-connect | DA:Pr6.1.IIIa; DA:Pr6.1.IIIb; DA:Cn10.1.IIIa; DA:Cn10.1.IIIb; DA:Cn11.1.IIIa | Performing (Pr6); connecting (Cn10, Cn11). Works: Trisha Brown, Roof Piece (United States, 1971, danced across New York rooftops; the company's page), described and linked: a dance made for a place; Pearl Primus, Hard Time Blues (United States, 1943, a solo on sharecroppers' lives; Primus 1919 to 1994, a dancer and anthropologist; a dance history archive's page), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: Level 2's production plan and concert roles (unit 10) and the research notes. DA:Cn10.1.IIIa: review the student's own choreography over the levels (the portfolio) and analyze what changed as they grew. DA:Cn10.1.IIIb: research dance careers (the federal labor statistics agency's page on dancers and choreographers, as of its date, and a working artist's own published words), choose one of interest and complete a capstone project on it. DA:Cn11.1.IIIa: study dances across eras (Primus 1943, Brown 1971 and the year's works), link their technique to their people's views, and note the student's own growth beside them. DA:Pr6.1.IIIa: lead rehearsals, model etiquette, build projection strategies and compile a portfolio documenting the process. DA:Pr6.1.IIIb: stage the concert in at least two places (for example the gym and a courtyard, or a stage and the library), designing and organizing the production for each. Words: venue, portfolio, growth, career, capstone, era. Showing: the Level 3 concerts for families and the school, with the family's yes, or the student's own at 18 or over. Evidence, id by id. Mid-unit assessment: DA:Cn10.1.IIIa, a kept piece: the growth review across the portfolio, drafted, given written feedback and revised; DA:Cn11.1.IIIa, a written item in a sitting: two dances of different eras, their technique linked to their people's views, with the student's growth noted. End-of-unit assessment (the concerts, then the capstone): DA:Pr6.1.IIIa, a look-for in a led rehearsal and a kept piece: the process portfolio; DA:Pr6.1.IIIb, a kept piece with a look-for: the production design for each venue and both concerts staged; DA:Cn10.1.IIIb, a kept piece: the career capstone, drafted, given written feedback and revised. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: Level 4 applies the Level 3 ids to the student's own series. Tools: the venue design sheet, the portfolio guide, the career research sheet, Curio Class's own. Free path: two spaces on the school grounds. Short path: 16 days | Last in Level 3 because the growth review needs the whole portfolio and the concerts show the year's refined work; the career-capstone model starts here and Level 4's capstone uses it. Its concerts in weeks 31 and 32 are its own end-of-unit assessment | Candidate: a site dance from Africa or Asia, described and linked | Hard Time Blues protests the treatment of Black sharecroppers: the sensitivity agent reads an archive's page and Primus's own published words first; studied by watching and reading, no role play. A career a student chooses may touch pay and access: facts come from the agency page with its date. Venue hazards and controls: each venue checked for floor, edges, exits and surface before rehearsal; outdoors, shade, water and no wet surface; an off-site venue follows the school's field trip rules; no rooftop is ever used. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
339: | 16 | Level 4 | The proposal and the series. *What series of dances do I want to make, and how will I know it is my best work?* | 70 | da-g12-studio | None | Creating (Cr1, Cr2, Cr3) on the student's own work; responding in critique. Works: chosen by the student from the band's works and their own research, two at least with one by a living maker, each with a work card and a rights line; teacher-set starting set: Crystal Pite, The Statement (Canada, 2016; the artist's page), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: Level 3's risk-taking, viewpoint statement and revision record (units 13 and 14). Owns no id: this year assesses the Level 3 creating ids again on new, self-directed work (DA:Cr1.1.IIIa, DA:Cr2.1.IIIa and DA:Cr3.1.IIIa, owned in Level 3, units 13 and 14), with a higher bar for independence. The student writes a proposal (an intent, sources, a series of two or three dances, a timeline), makes the series with checkpoints, keeps the revision record, and writes a defended artist's statement for the series. Words: proposal, series, self-directed, studio, checkpoint, portfolio. Showing: each dance of the series at its checkpoint, for the class. Evidence: mid-unit checkpoints (the proposal approved after written feedback, about day 10; the first dance shown, about day 40) and the end-of-unit assessment: DA:Cr1.1.IIIa, a look-for on the series: blended sources and a personal voice carrying the proposal's intent; DA:Cr2.1.IIIa, a look-for and a kept piece: original dances with choices justified in the defended statement; DA:Cr3.1.IIIa, a kept piece: the revision record for the series, every change defended. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet on the Level 3 guide with the higher bar. Tools: the proposal frame, the studio log, the checkpoint sheet, Curio Class's own. Free path: as unit 3 | First in Level 4 because the year is one sustained project and the series is what units 17 to 19 critique, stage and connect; 70 days because the series is two or three dances made, critiqued and revised by the student, and the mid-year checkpoint follows it | Candidate: a fourth checkpoint day for students whose series has three dances | The student chooses the subject; a personal subject is invited, never required, with an invented or public subject marked the same. Health's steps if a student tells something. Hazards and controls: as unit 12; the student choreographs within the class safety rules, and the teacher keeps the stop cue. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
340: | 17 | Level 4 | The critic's year. *What makes a critique fair and strong, and can I critique my own series?* | 24 | da-g12-criticism | None | Responding (Re7, Re8, Re9). Works: three genres for the critique set: Mark Morris, Dido and Aeneas (United States, 1989, baroque opera danced; the company's page); Sankai Juku, Hibiki (Japan, 1998, butoh; the company's page); Crystal Pite's The Statement (unit 16) or a work of the student's choice; each described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: Level 3's critique and taste defense (unit 14). Owns no id: this year assesses the Level 3 responding ids again on new works (DA:Re7.1.IIIa, DA:Re8.1.IIIa and DA:Re9.1.IIIa, owned in Level 3, unit 14), with a higher bar for evidence. The student writes a critique set on three dances of different genres, citing evidence and stated criteria, and a critique of their own series, then defends one critique with peers. Words: critique, evidence, criteria, genre, taste, fair. Showing: the critiques shared in a peer panel. Evidence: a mid-unit checkpoint (the first critique, given written feedback, about day 10) and the end-of-unit assessment: DA:Re7.1.IIIa, a kept piece: patterns and structure analyzed in each critique; DA:Re8.1.IIIa, a kept piece: elements, principles and context interpreted in each; DA:Re9.1.IIIa, a kept piece and a spoken item: tastes named, values weighed and one critique defended in the panel. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet on the Level 3 guide with the higher bar. Tools: the critique frame, the evidence log, the panel card, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencil | After unit 16 because the student's own series is one of the critiques; before unit 18 because the critique of the series shapes its final rehearsals | Candidate: a fourth genre from Africa or the Americas, described and linked | Butoh may show bodies painted white and slow, intense images: the teacher previews the linked material; the sensitivity agent reads the company's own words first. Critique names the work, never the dancer's body. Hazards and controls: as unit 1 (seated and moving review days). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
341: | 18 | Level 4 | Leading the showing. *How does a choreographer lead a cast and a production to an audience?* | 40 | da-g12-perform | None | Performing (Pr4, Pr5, Pr6). Works: the student's own series; Pina Bausch's Café Müller (unit 13) revisited for staging in a found space | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: Level 3's projection strategies, led rehearsal and venue design (units 12 and 15). Owns no id: this year assesses the Level 3 performing ids again on the student's own new work (DA:Pr4.1.IIIa, DA:Pr5.1.IIIa and DA:Pr6.1.IIIa, owned in Level 3, units 12 and 15), with a higher bar for leadership. The student leads rehearsals of the series with a cast of classmates (or performs it as a solo where they choose), plans the space, the production needs and the practice, and stages a showing for an audience. Words: cast, call, run, production needs, self-evaluate, lead. Showing: the series showing for families and the school, with the family's yes, or the student's own at 18 or over; only original, public domain or openly licensed dance is shown. Evidence: a mid-unit checkpoint (the rehearsal plan and a led rehearsal, about day 20) and the end-of-unit assessment: DA:Pr4.1.IIIa, a look-for on the showing: the full spatial range toward the audience; DA:Pr5.1.IIIa, a look-for and a kept piece: body-mind principles applied and the student's self-evaluation; DA:Pr6.1.IIIa, a kept piece and a look-for: the rehearsal record, the led rehearsals and the production. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet on the Level 3 guide with the higher bar. Tools: the rehearsal record, the production plan, the call sheet, Curio Class's own. Free path: as unit 15 | After unit 17 because the critique of the series shapes its final rehearsals; before the year-end checkpoint (week 28) so a senior who leaves early has shown the series | Candidate: a cast-care card on how a student leader gives notes | A student leading classmates gives notes on the work, never on a dancer's body; the teacher keeps the stop cue and the safety rules. Hazards and controls: as units 12 and 15. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
342: | 19 | Level 4 | The capstone. *Where does my work sit in dance's history, and where will I take it?* | 22 | da-g12-connect | None | Connecting (Cn10, Cn11). Works: the student's series beside one dance era of their choice from the band's works (for example Fuller's 1890s, Primus's 1940s or Brown's 1970s) and one community's view, each with its work card | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: Level 3's growth review and career capstone (unit 15). Owns no id: this year assesses the Level 3 connecting ids again on new work (DA:Cn10.1.IIIa and DA:Cn11.1.IIIa, owned in Level 3, unit 15). The student ties the series to a dance era, a community's view and their own growth over the levels, and gives a short capstone talk with a written link to context. Words: era, context, growth, capstone, viewpoint, research. Showing: the capstone talks for families and the school. Evidence: a mid-unit checkpoint (the written link to context, given written feedback, about day 10) and the end-of-unit assessment: DA:Cn10.1.IIIa, a kept piece and a spoken item: the growth across the portfolio, in the written link and the talk; DA:Cn11.1.IIIa, a spoken item and a kept piece: the series' technique linked to the chosen era and a community's view. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet on the Level 3 guide. Tools: the capstone frame, the talk card, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencil, the portfolio folder | Last in the band because it looks back over the series and the levels; it follows the year-end checkpoint, and its talk is its own end-of-unit assessment. A senior who leaves early gives the talk in week 30 | Candidate: a recorded-talk version on the family's device for a student who will not speak to a group | A community's view comes from its own published words; a student names only a heritage they choose to name. Health's steps if a student tells something. Hazards and controls: as unit 1 (mostly seated). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
374: | Unit 2 (Level 1 course days 13 to 50, weeks 3 to 10): performance quality and rehearsal of partner and group phrases (da-g9-technique) | physical-education:g9-dance-design: physical education grades 9 to 12, grade 9 unit 5, "Dance by design" (days 53 to 64 of its grade 9 year, weeks 22 to 26): a set sequence learned and a short phrase designed, as movement skill | The dance unit teaches the counts and the phrase it dances itself from the bridge; it teaches no motor cue. Boundary: physical education teaches the sequence and dance forms as movement skill, this course teaches choreography, performing for an audience and response; each unit names the other in its connections line, so nothing is taught twice in a year. |

