216: | 1 | Wellness and a goal. *How do the parts of a person's wellness work together, and what one goal is worth a semester?* | 4 | g9-phw-strengths-wellness-links; g9-phw-goal-assess-write | NHES.S1.12.1; NHES.S1.12.2; NHES.S6.12.1; NHES.S6.12.2; NHES.S6.12.3 | Setting goals (standard 6): the goal-plan at span-12 depth | Day 1 (hook: a character with a new job, a sport and a family duty whose sleep, mood and friendships move together): how the parts of wellness (physical, mental and emotional, social, and the setting around a person) connect to health results (NHES.S1.12.2); retrieval of the grade 8 goal log and factor levels. Day 2: ways to build strengths that help one person and a group (a household's shared sleep routine, a team's water habit, a friend group that walks), applied in a plan for a character and for a group the student chooses (NHES.S1.12.1). Day 3: assess one's own health and the factors that bear on a goal, on a sheet that stays with the student (NHES.S6.12.1); choose a goal process alone, with support or together, and why it fits (NHES.S6.12.2); write a goal from the safe list or the student's own healthy behavior (never weight, shape, food restriction or calories) with how it supports health (NHES.S6.12.3); the goal log starts. Day 4: end-of-unit check on an unseen case: how two parts of a character's wellness connect, a strength that helps a group, the character's goal written with its process and health link; the student's own goal sheet is checked for its parts only (or the school-day goal). Out: carrying out and evaluating the goal (the weekly slots and unit 16). Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO1, HBO2, HBO7. Tools: the goal log (one paper page a week). Curio Class writes: two cases, the goal log, the goal-plan step card at span-12 depth. Cost (class of 30, provisional): $4 printing | First because the goal thread needs a written goal by day 4 so the first weekly slot (week 2) can start, and the wellness links give the goal its reason. Retrieval: g8-phw-factors-shape-health, g8-phw-goal-monitor and g8-phw-goal-effect (health grade 8), the year before | Candidate: HECAT PHW module 9 to 12 read may add teen sleep amounts; one sourced fact line inside day 1 | The self-assessment and the goal stay with the student; never ask about a student's own health, body or family. Goals from the safe list or the student's own healthy behavior, never about weight, shape, food restriction or calories (APPROACH.md, Supports); a character's goal is always an option, and a school-day goal stands for the doing record. Goals fit any body and any condition. Teacher's own views note (bodies, food, family). Agent reads: sensitivity (students with chronic conditions and disabilities; a national eating disorders association's guidance for schools) |
217: | 2 | What gets in the way. *Why does a teen who wants to be more active often not do it?* | 4 | g9-phw-factors-and-behavior | NHES.S1.12.6; NHES.S1.12.7; NHES.S1.12.8 | Health information (standard 1) with the influence check at four levels | Day 1 (norm: the latest CDC Youth Risk Behavior Survey share of high school students active at least 60 minutes every day, source and year at the expert read): how factors at the personal, social, community and policy levels connect and together shape a result (NHES.S1.12.6), on a physical activity case (a character who wants to walk to school or join a team; grade 8 used sleep and school start time, so this case is new). Day 2: what helps and what gets in the way of a behavior, sorted by level (NHES.S1.12.7). Day 3: how confidence in one's own ability and one's sense of risk change a behavior, with the character's two tries (NHES.S1.12.8). Day 4: end-of-unit check on an unseen activity case: map its factors and how they connect, name two barriers and one help, explain how confidence and risk sense would change the behavior. Out: training principles and fitness (PE). Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO6, HBO7. Tools: the four-level factor map. Curio Class writes: the case in two parts, a factor map sheet. Cost: $3 | Second because the four-level factor map is the tool units 3, 7 and 13 use, and students meet it before their first goal slot (week 2). Retrieval: g8-phw-factors-shape-health (health grade 8), the year before. PE grades 9 to 12 teaches the moving (Cross-subject) | Candidate: HECAT PA module 9 to 12 read may add muscle and bone activity guidance; one sourced line inside day 1 | No ranking of students' own activity, body size or fitness; a body of any size, ability or condition can be active; a student who uses a wheelchair or cannot run is in every case's range. Agent reads: sensitivity (students with disabilities and chronic conditions) |
218: | 3 | Fair chances. *Why do two neighborhoods a few miles apart not have the same health?* | 3 | g9-phw-conditions-equity | NHES.S2.12.2; NHES.S2.12.3 | Analyzing influences (standard 2): the influence check at the community and environment levels | Day 1: two neighborhoods' data cards (invented for practice: parks, grocery stores, clinic hours, bus routes, air quality, average income) and one real agency figure on a health gap by income or place, with source and year (picked at the unit plan from a federal or state health data page, checked at the expert read): how social conditions such as income and neighborhood shape behaviors, results and fairness in health (NHES.S2.12.2). Day 2: appraise how factors from the personal to the environmental affect fairness, and what each level can change (NHES.S2.12.3), with community strengths and action named as well as gaps. Day 3: end-of-unit check on an unseen pair of places: assess a gap and appraise which levels shape it. Rates and percents only as grade 6 and 7 math taught them. Out: blame of a person or a group; any student's own neighborhood. Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO6. Curio Class writes: data cards, a fairness map. Cost: $4 | After unit 2's four levels, which it applies to places. Retrieval: g8-phw-factors-shape-health, the year before | Candidate: HECAT PHW module 9 to 12 read may add environmental health expectations; one data card | Families and neighborhoods in the room vary: study places, not people; no student describes their own neighborhood, income or insurance; no deficit framing of a community, and every place is shown with its strengths. Agent reads: sensitivity (students and families with low income, and neighborhoods named in public data) |
219: | 4 | Getting care. *What makes it easy or hard for a teen to reach health care and information they can trust?* | 4 | g9-phw-access-to-care | NHES.S1.12.9; NHES.S3.12.1; NHES.S3.12.2 | Accessing information and services (standard 3): help-steps and source-check | Day 1: how reaching care relates to wellness (a character's untreated earache, a missed checkup, a delayed eye exam) (NHES.S1.12.9). Day 2: how easy it is to reach trusted adults, professionals and resources, mapped on an invented town and on the school's own list (nurse, counselor, a clinic, a pharmacy, help lines named by purpose) (NHES.S3.12.1). Day 3: what opens or blocks access to trustworthy information and services: cost, insurance, transport, hours, language, privacy, and confidential care, whose rules for teens vary by state (the school adds its state's rule; no legal advice) (NHES.S3.12.2). Day 4: end-of-unit check on an unseen case: map the character's options and blocks and pick a route with a reason; readiness check on g7-phw-living-with-conditions in the last ten minutes. Out: insurance plan details. Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO10, HBO11. Curio Class writes: the town map, the school-list template, access cards. Cost: $3 | A getting-help unit in a non-sensitive place, so units 19 and 21 can be left out. After unit 3, because cost and place are the blocks unit 3 studied. Retrieval: g8-phw-health-care-services (health grade 8), the year before | Candidate: HECAT PHW module 9 to 12 read may add "know your health history and how to share it"; one scene inside day 2 | Access and cost differ; no student is asked about their own insurance, care or health history. Confidential care is a fact about rights whose details vary by state, never advice. Agent reads: sensitivity (students and families without insurance or with low income) |
220: | 5 | Habits that prevent. *Which habits really cut down illness and injury, and how do people live well with an ongoing condition?* | 3 | g9-phw-evaluate-habits | NHES.S1.12.3; NHES.S1.12.4 | Health information (standard 1): evaluating evidence with the goal-plan's look-back | Day 1: evaluate behaviors for how well they prevent or cut down illness and injury (seat belts, helmets, hand washing, vaccines, sunscreen, sleep), each with its agency source and year (NHES.S1.12.3). Day 2: evaluate habits that support health, including care of an ongoing condition (an asthma action plan, diabetes care at school, a seizure plan), with characters who live full lives and name their supports (NHES.S1.12.4); the readiness results from unit 4 shape day 2's opening. Day 3: end-of-unit check on an unseen set of habits: rank and justify how well each prevents harm, and judge a character's condition-care routine. Out: treatment choices and medical advice. Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO5, HBO6, HBO9. Curio Class writes: habit cards with sources, three condition-care cases. Cost: $3 | After unit 4, because condition care uses the care map. The readiness check sits on unit 4's last day because g7-phw-living-with-conditions was last used in grade 7 (health grades 6 to 8), two grades back. Retrieval: g8-phw-habits-personal-community, the year before. Biology grade 9 runs the same year; health states its own plain facts with sources (Cross-subject) | Candidate: HECAT PHW module 9 to 12 read may add self-exams and screenings by age; one sourced line inside day 1 | No blame for illness or injury; a student with a condition is never singled out or asked to explain it. Vaccines: plain facts, no debate staged, a family's choices never discussed. Agent reads: sensitivity (students with chronic conditions and disabilities; CDC chronic disease and vaccine pages) |
221: | 6 | Information you can trust. *Should a teen athlete buy a protein powder, and what do I do about a post that says so?* | 4 | g9-fn-information-skill; g9-meh-counter-disinformation | NHES.S1.12.5; NHES.S3.12.3; NHES.S3.12.4; NHES.S3.12.5 | Accessing valid information (standard 3): source-check, with the speak-up plan's counter line | Day 1: how the health information skills of people and organizations shape results (a clear clinic handout against a confusing one, a label read or misread) (NHES.S1.12.5). Day 2: rate resources on accuracy, trustworthiness and availability on one food and supplement question, whether a teen athlete needs a protein powder (NIH and FDA supplement pages and the federal dietary guidelines, latest edition, as the anchor sources; that supplements are not approved for effect before sale is a fact checked at the expert read) (NHES.S3.12.3); choose the accurate sources and use them to answer the question (NHES.S3.12.4). Day 3: misinformation and deliberate disinformation about mental health, drugs, diets or wellness trends, on invented posts: pause, trace, counter kindly, report (NHES.S3.12.5). Day 4: end-of-unit check on an unseen question and source set: rate, choose and answer with the sources used, and write a counter to one invented post. Out: any product name; diet advice. Serves HECAT.FN.HBO1, HBO8; PHW.HBO12; MEH.HBO2, HBO9. Tools: printed source packets (class devices optional). Curio Class writes: source packets, rating sheet, invented posts. Cost: $5 | After unit 4's source-check on care, which this unit deepens. Before unit 7, which judges media among influences. Retrieval: g8-phw-handle-false-messages (health grade 8) and the argument-and-evidence reading of ELA grade 8 unit 2 (RI.8.8), the year before | Candidate: HECAT FN module 9 to 12 read may add energy drink and caffeine expectations; one source card | No product or brand names; posts are invented. No student is asked about their own supplement use, diet or body. No calories, no weight, no "good" or "bad" foods. Agent reads: sensitivity (students with eating disorders or body image concerns; a national eating disorders association's guidance for schools) |
222: | 7 | Influences and my plan. *What pulls on a teen's well-being, and what plan helps manage it?* | 4 | g9-meh-influences-judge-plan | NHES.S2.12.1; NHES.S2.12.4; NHES.S2.12.5 | Analyzing influences (standard 2): the influence check turned into a plan | Day 1: judge how influences and behaviors act on each other and on well-being (late-night scrolling and sleep, stress and caffeine, a friend group's habits) on a character's week (NHES.S2.12.1). Day 2: design a plan to handle the influences on health, for the character and, if the student chooses, privately for themselves (NHES.S2.12.4). Day 3: use the resources available to manage an influence (NHES.S2.12.5): each student uses one school resource from unit 4's map on a plan step (the school's help directory, a planner, a quiet space, a study-break routine) and logs that it was used, never why; the teacher records the use. Day 4: end-of-unit check on an unseen case: judge the influences and write a plan with one resource. Out: diagnosis of any mental illness. Serves HECAT.MEH.HBO2, HBO4, HBO9. Curio Class writes: the character's week, plan sheet. Cost: $3 | After units 2, 3 and 6, whose levels and source-check feed the influence judgment. Before unit 8, where asking for help is one of the resources. Retrieval: g8-meh-tools-for-influences, the year before | Candidate: HECAT MEH module 9 to 12 read may add stress management expectations; one line inside day 2 | Stress is a normal part of life and never equated with illness; no student is asked about their own mental health or feelings; the personal plan stays with the student. Agent reads: sensitivity |
223: | 8 | Reaching people. *How do I ask for help, or show I care, in a text, an email or face to face?* | 4 | g9-meh-communication-channels; g9-meh-help-and-concern | NHES.S4.12.1; NHES.S4.12.2; NHES.S4.12.3; NHES.S4.12.10 | Communicating (standard 4): the I-statement and listening steps across channels, with the help-steps | Day 1: communicate through many channels and media (talk, text, a post, an email, a clinic portal message): what each is good for, tone, privacy and permanence (NHES.S4.12.1). Day 2: use the skills across relationships: a friend, a manager about a shift that cuts into sleep, a doctor's office (NHES.S4.12.2), drafted on paper templates never sent. Day 3: ask for and give help for health (NHES.S4.12.3), and express understanding and concern for a friend who is stressed, sad or worried: notice, check in, listen, offer, and know when to tell an adult (NHES.S4.12.10); help lines named by purpose, including 988 for anyone in emotional distress by call, text or chat. Day 4: end-of-unit check on unseen scenarios in two channels and two relationships: a written help request and a concern response; the spoken rehearsal record from days 3 and 4 is carried. Out: suicide and self-harm content (unit 21). Serves HECAT.MEH.HBO1, HBO4, HBO8. Curio Class writes: message templates, scenario cards, the help-line card. Cost: $3 | The getting-help unit of the course in a non-sensitive place, so unit 21 can be left out; unit 21 extends it. After unit 7, which named help as a resource. Retrieval: g8-meh-teamwork and g8-meh-express-concern, the year before | Candidate: HECAT MEH module 9 to 12 read may add "help a friend reach a counselor"; one scene inside day 3 | Friend scenarios are characters'; no student is asked about their own struggles. If a student tells you something: listen, do not promise to keep it secret, follow the school's duty to report, and tell the student who the next adult is. No real message is sent. Agent reads: sensitivity |
224: | 9 | Teams and other views. *How does a team get a health job done when its members want different things?* | 4 | g9-meh-teams-negotiate-views | NHES.S4.12.7; NHES.S4.12.8; NHES.S4.12.9 | Communicating (standard 4): listening steps and I-statement in teamwork and negotiation | Day 1: teamwork in many settings (a sports team, a job, a class project, a household) and the roles that get a job done (NHES.S4.12.7); teams of four form and keep together for units 17 and 18. Day 2: negotiation and compromise in different settings: say what each side needs, offer trades, agree on a fair plan (NHES.S4.12.8). Day 3: change how I communicate to suit people with other views and values: a food custom, a family's screen rules, a team's training choice, worded to stay neutral on beliefs (NHES.S4.12.9). Day 4: end-of-unit check: each team plans a short health task while the teacher records each student's teamwork and negotiation moves on a record sheet, plus an unseen written scenario on adapting a message. Out: choosing the advocacy issue (unit 17). Serves HECAT.MEH.HBO7, HBO8. Curio Class writes: role cards, negotiation scenes. Cost: $3 | After unit 8's channels, which teams use. Before the mid-course check; the teams formed here run units 17 and 18. Retrieval: g8-meh-teamwork and g8-meh-different-views, the year before | Candidate: HECAT MEH module 9 to 12 read may add "respect differences" expectations; one scene inside day 3 | Values scenes stay neutral on faith, culture and family; no student is asked to defend or reveal their own beliefs; teams are set by the teacher so no student is left out. Agent reads: sensitivity (students of every faith and culture) |
225: | 10 | Limits, consent and conflict. *How do I state a limit, give or take back a yes, and settle a conflict before it turns violent?* | 4 | g9-meh-boundaries-consent; g9-v-conflict-manage | NHES.S4.12.4; NHES.S4.12.6 | Communicating (standard 4): boundary steps and conflict steps | Day 1: state limits, give or take back consent and set boundaries in friendships, at work, online and in shared spaces (a shared photo, a borrowed car ride, an extra shift, a group chat): a yes can be changed, and asking before sharing (NHES.S4.12.4); the readiness results on g7-s-online-boundaries shape the opening. Day 2: boundary rehearsal in pairs with scripts. Day 3: prevent, manage and settle conflicts: calm, listen, negotiate, stay out of places where violence is likely, walk away from people who urge it, get help; no violence is an option (NHES.S4.12.6). Day 4: end-of-unit check on unseen scenarios: the boundary steps and the conflict steps written for characters; the spoken rehearsal record from days 3 and 4 is carried. Out: sexual consent and dating violence (units 19 and 20). Serves HECAT.MEH.HBO3, HBO8; V.HBO1, HBO2, HBO5, HBO6. Curio Class writes: boundary and conflict scripts, scenario cards. Cost: $3 | After the mid-course check. The non-sensitive home of the consent and conflict skills, so units 19 and 20 can be left out and extend it. The readiness check on g7-s-online-boundaries (health grade 7, two grades back) sits in the last ten minutes of the mid-course respond day. Retrieval: g8-v-negotiate-compromise, the year before | Candidate: HECAT V module 9 to 12 read may add online harassment expectations; one scene inside day 1 | No sexual content in this unit. Conflict scenes use scripts and stay verbal; no student acts out a fight or plays an aggressor; a card plays any pressure. Agent reads: sensitivity |
226: | 11 | Nicotine decisions. *When does a vaping choice deserve a careful process, and who can help?* | 4 | g9-t-decision-views-process | NHES.S5.12.1; NHES.S5.12.2; NHES.S5.12.3 | Deciding (standard 5): the decision steps as a careful process, alone, with support or in a group | Day 1 (norm: the latest National Youth Tobacco Survey share of high school students who used e-cigarettes in the past 30 days; most do not): how a nicotine decision looks to different people and communities (a teen, a parent who smokes, a coach, a store owner, a neighborhood with many tobacco ads) (NHES.S5.12.1). Day 2: determine when and why a situation needs a careful decision process (NHES.S5.12.2). Day 3: use the process alone, with support and in a group on a vaping decision, with where to get help to quit: a free national quit line and a federal text program, named by purpose (NHES.S5.12.3). Day 4: end-of-unit check on an unseen nicotine case: say why it needs a careful process and run it. Out: shock images, worst cases. Serves HECAT.T.HBO1, HBO3, HBO5. Curio Class writes: viewpoint cards, decision sheet, invented flavored-product ads with no brand. Cost: $3 | After unit 10's limits, which a refusal inside a decision uses, and before unit 12, which reuses the decision steps on alcohol and drugs. Retrieval: g8-aod-decision-supports-obstacles and g8-t-tobacco-influences, the year before | Candidate: HECAT T module 9 to 12 read may add nicotine pouch expectations; one source card | No blame of a family member who smokes; no student who uses nicotine is singled out, and quit help is offered to the whole class. Pictures only. Agent reads: sensitivity (students who use nicotine or live with someone who does) |
227: | 12 | Alcohol and other drugs. *How do I say no at a party, in a car or at work, and carry out a choice I have made?* | 4 | g9-aod-say-no-across; g9-aod-weigh-options-plan | NHES.S4.12.5; NHES.S5.12.4; NHES.S5.12.6 | Communicating (standard 4): refusal steps across circumstances; deciding (standard 5): weighing options and an action plan | Day 1 (norm: the latest Youth Risk Behavior Survey share of high school students who drank alcohol in the past 30 days; most did not): weigh options against priorities and likely results for an alcohol or drug decision (NHES.S5.12.4); medicine safety (a prescription is for its own person and is never shared; pills not from a pharmacy can contain fentanyl; naloxone can reverse an opioid overdose, and rules on carrying it vary by state), facts checked at the expert read. Day 2: ways to say no across circumstances (a party, a ride, a job, a partner or friend who asks you to ride or use), including never driving after alcohol or other drugs and not riding with someone who has, with a safe-ride plan (NHES.S4.12.5), rehearsed in pairs with scripts. Day 3: create an action plan to carry out a decision, with where to get help to stop use (a free national helpline named by purpose) (NHES.S5.12.6). Day 4: end-of-unit check on unseen scenes: a refusal for two circumstances, options weighed and an action plan for a character; the spoken refusal record from days 3 and 4 is carried. Out: drug-by-drug detail. Serves HECAT.AOD.HBO1 to HBO8; S.HBO2; MEH.HBO5. Curio Class writes: circumstance cards, refusal scripts with a card playing the offer, an action plan sheet. Cost: $3 | After unit 11's careful process, which it reuses. Retrieval: g8-aod-refusal-and-driving and g8-pa-decision-look-back, the year before | Candidate: HECAT AOD module 9 to 12 read may add cannabis and state law expectations; one sourced line inside day 1 | No student is asked about their own or their family's use; no scare images or worst cases as the norm; a card plays the offer and no student plays the person offering. Agent reads: sensitivity (students who use alcohol or drugs, or live with someone who does; SAMHSA family pages) |
228: | 13 | Safety at many levels. *How do one driver's choice, a family's rules and a state law each change who gets hurt on the road and at work?* | 3 | g9-s-decision-levels-barriers | NHES.S5.12.5; NHES.S5.12.7 | Deciding (standard 5): decision steps with the influence check's levels | Day 1: how a decision could affect health at several levels (a new driver's phone use and passengers: self, riders, other road users, the community) (NHES.S5.12.5), with the national highway traffic safety agency's teen driving pages. Day 2: judge how supports and barriers shape decisions at various levels: graduated licensing rules, a family driving agreement, a first job's safety rules on burns, lifting and hours (federal Department of Labor youth employment pages) (NHES.S5.12.7). Day 3: end-of-unit check on an unseen case (a home or workplace safety choice): trace its effects by level and judge its supports and barriers; readiness check on g6-fn-eating-patterns in the last ten minutes. Out: driver education. Serves HECAT.S.HBO1, HBO3, HBO4, HBO5, HBO6, HBO8. Curio Class writes: two cases, a level chart. Cost: $3 | After units 11 and 12's decision steps, and before unit 14, whose readiness check it holds. Retrieval: g8-pa-decision-look-back, the year before | Candidate: HECAT S module 9 to 12 read may add first aid and CPR awareness; one line naming where a school can add training | No crash images; a student who has lost someone on the road or at work is given a way out of the case. Agent reads: sensitivity (students who have lost someone on the road or at work) |
229: | 14 | Meals that work for a group. *How can a household or a team eat well on a small budget?* | 3 | g9-fn-habits-groups | NHES.S7.12.2; NHES.S7.12.4 | Practicing healthy behaviors (standard 7) with the goal-plan | Day 1: judge how habits and other factors (time, money, culture, who cooks, school meals) support wellness for people and groups (NHES.S7.12.2); the readiness results on g6-fn-eating-patterns shape the opening. Day 2: show a range of habits that support a group's wellness (NHES.S7.12.4): plan three shared meals on a set budget from an invented store flyer (unit prices as grade 6 math taught them), and each student carries out one habit in class, recorded by the teacher: hand washing and an optional no-cook assembly (a bean and vegetable wrap or a fruit and yogurt cup, under the school's food and allergy rules), or reading a label and portioning a dish to share. Day 3: end-of-unit check on an unseen household case: judge its habits and plan a budget week of shared meals; the day 2 record is carried. Out: weighing, calories, diets. Serves HECAT.FN.HBO1, HBO8, HBO10, HBO11. Curio Class writes: the store flyer (invented for practice), meal plan sheet, no-cook card. Cost: $3 printing; the optional no-cook demonstration about $45 for 30 students; soap and towels from school stock | After unit 13, which holds its readiness check because g6-fn-eating-patterns was last used in grade 6 (health grades 6 to 8), three grades back. Before unit 15, which adapts habits. Retrieval: g8-phw-habits-personal-community, the year before | Candidate: HECAT FN module 9 to 12 read may add food safety at home expectations; one line inside day 2 | Food: never ask what a student eats at home; budgets, cultures and school meals vary and are all respected; no good or bad foods, no weighing, no calories. Food demonstration: allergies checked first, no knives (pre-cut items), hand washing, five minutes of clean-up; any student may watch or plan instead of eating. Agent reads: sensitivity (students with low income or too little food at home; students with eating disorders); safety (food handling, allergies) |
230: | 15 | When habits get harder. *What makes a healthy habit easier or harder, and how do I know whether a plan is working?* | 4 | g9-pa-habits-ease-adapt; g9-pa-measure-decisions | NHES.S7.12.1; NHES.S7.12.3; NHES.S5.12.8 | Practicing healthy behaviors (standard 7) and deciding (standard 5): the goal-plan and decision steps with simple data | Day 1: assess what makes healthy behaviors easier or harder (time, place, people, money, mood) on a character's activity plan (NHES.S7.12.1). Day 2: measure how well a health decision worked with simple data: a character's one-month activity plan record (invented for practice) and the student's own goal log (planned against done days, a barrier, a change), counted and charted (NHES.S5.12.8); monitoring and revising the activity plan is the goal process in the physical activity topic, practice of NHES.S6.12.5, owned in unit 16. Day 3: change a habit as personal and group needs change (a new job, a new season, an injury): each student revises one step of their goal or school-day goal in the log, with the reason, recorded (NHES.S7.12.3). Day 4: end-of-unit check on an unseen character's data: assess what made it harder, measure how the plan worked, and adapt it. Out: training principles and fitness testing (PE). Serves HECAT.PA.HBO1, HBO2, HBO5, HBO6, HBO7. Curio Class writes: the month record, a chart sheet. Cost: $3 | Late in the goal thread (weeks 11 and 12), so the goal log holds ten weekly entries to measure. Before unit 16, which evaluates the goal. PE grades 9 to 12 teaches training principles and moving (Cross-subject). Retrieval: g8-phw-habits-personal-community and g8-pa-decision-look-back, the year before | Candidate: HECAT PA module 9 to 12 read may add "plan activity for life after school"; one line inside day 3 | Data are about the plan, never about the body: no weight, fitness test score or calories; injury and disability are in the cases' range. Agent reads: sensitivity (students with disabilities, injuries or eating disorders) |
231: | 16 | My goal, looked back. *What did ten weeks of my goal do for my health, and what do I keep?* | 2 | g9-phw-goal-carry-revise | NHES.S6.12.4; NHES.S6.12.5; NHES.S6.12.6 | Setting goals (standard 6): the goal-plan's last steps | Carry out a plan that deals with supports and barriers (NHES.S6.12.4) and monitor progress and revise the goal or plan (NHES.S6.12.5): taught and practiced in the ten-minute weekly goal slots, weeks 2 to 11, each slot one step (a step done, a barrier met, a support used, a revision with its reason), written in the goal log. Day 1: gather the log; evaluate how the goal process went and what it did for health and well-being, and what to carry into the next goal (NHES.S6.12.6). Day 2: end-of-unit check: the student's goal log (its process entries; the goal's content may be covered) and a written evaluation, scored on the process steps, never on whether the goal worked or on health; plus an unseen character's log to evaluate. Out: none left; the thread ends here. Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO2, HBO7. Curio Class writes: the slot prompts (ten), the evaluation sheet. Cost: $2 | Last of the goal thread, after unit 15 measured and revised the plan; the log is the doing record for NHES.S6.12.4 and NHES.S6.12.5. Retrieval: g8-phw-goal-effect and g8-phw-goal-monitor, the year before | Candidate: HECAT PHW module 9 to 12 read may add a long-term health plan expectation; one line inside day 1 | No grade on whether the goal worked; a goal that did not work is information, not failure. A student who kept the goal private submits the school-day goal log. Agent reads: sensitivity |
232: | 17 | Taking up a health issue. *Which health issue at our school is worth our team's effort, and how do I speak up for my own health needs?* | 4 | g9-s-advocacy-issue | NHES.S8.12.1; NHES.S8.12.2; NHES.S8.12.4 | Advocacy (standard 8): the speak-up plan | Day 1: the factors that shape advocacy at every level (who decides, rules, money, public opinion, timing) (NHES.S8.12.1). Day 2: speak up for one's own health needs (asking a clinic a question, asking for a school accommodation, telling a coach about an injury), rehearsed with a character's script (NHES.S8.12.4). Day 3: teams from unit 9 take up one school health issue from a short list (shade at the bus stop, water stations, safe walking routes, a quiet space, a later practice start) and draft their ask with a claim, reasons and evidence (NHES.S8.12.2; the argument writing of ELA grade 8 unit 2, W.8.1, retrieved); written feedback from the teacher overnight. Day 4: end-of-unit check: an unseen self-advocacy scene written alone, and a factor map of the team's issue; the team's draft is carried into unit 18. Out: delivering the ask (unit 18). Serves HECAT.S.HBO8; V.HBO7. Curio Class writes: the issue list, factor map, self-advocacy scripts. Cost: $4 | After units 9 (teams) and 2 and 3 (levels), and the last third of the semester so teams have their issue before unit 18 delivers it. Retrieval: g8-s-advocacy-results (health grade 8) and ELA grade 8 unit 2's argument writing (W.8.1), the year before | Candidate: HECAT S module 9 to 12 read may add "advocate for safe school environments"; it fits the issue list | Issues come from the short list and never target a person or a student; the teacher arranges real audiences. A self-advocacy scene is a character's. Agent reads: sensitivity (students with disabilities who use accommodations) |
233: | 18 | Advocacy that reaches. *How do we fit our ask to the audience, deliver it, and judge how far it reached?* | 4 | g9-v-advocacy-adjust; g9-t-advocacy-levels-assess | NHES.S8.12.3; NHES.S8.12.5; NHES.S8.12.6; NHES.S8.12.7 | Advocacy (standard 8): the speak-up plan adjusted and assessed | Day 1: revise the team's ask after the written feedback and adjust it for two groups and settings: a one-page ask to a school leader and a short talk or poster for peers (NHES.S8.12.3). Day 2: deliver to a real audience advocacy that reaches relationships, community, society and the environment: a bystander plan for friends, the school policy ask, and an ask for cleaner air or more shade near school (NHES.S8.12.5); the teacher records each team's delivery. Day 3: assess how the advocacy was carried out, what it achieved and how far it reached (NHES.S8.12.6), and examine how a varied group of community members worked together on a local tobacco-free policy, from a public case (a city or county tobacco-free parks or retail rule, with its source and date) (NHES.S8.12.7). Day 4: end-of-unit check: each student's written assessment of the team's reach and an unseen coalition case. Out: running a campaign beyond the school. Serves HECAT.V.HBO3, HBO7; T.HBO3, HBO4. Curio Class writes: the coalition case, delivery record, reach sheet. Cost: $6 (posters) | After unit 17's drafted ask; last non-sensitive unit, so the delivery is the course's last product. Retrieval: g8-fn-advocacy-audience and g8-pa-advocacy-practice, the year before | Candidate: HECAT T module 9 to 12 read may add tobacco-free policy expectations; it fits day 3 | No student must speak in public: a written ask or poster counts. The bystander plan is about characters and stays general. Agent reads: sensitivity |
234: | 19 | Sexual health and consent (sensitive, removable). *How do people keep relationships healthy and respectful, prevent infection and pregnancy, and find confidential care?* | 4 | g9-sh-health-and-consent | | Communicating (standard 4) and accessing services (standard 3): boundary steps and help-steps practiced (no new skill) | Revisits the grade 6 to 8 outcomes in one unit. Day 1: healthy relationships and consent: freely given, specific, can be taken back, cannot be given when someone is drunk, high or pressured. Day 2: preventing infection and pregnancy, medically accurate at the HECAT 9 to 12 expectation: not having sex is the only certain way; condoms and other contraception, how each works and how well; HIV and other infections, testing and treatment (CDC and federal family planning pages, latest edition). Day 3: confidential health services and how to reach them, practicing help-steps and the access map of unit 4. Day 4: end-of-unit check on unseen scenarios: consent and a written refusal or check-in line, and finding a confidential service for a character. Practices NHES.S4.12.4 (owned in unit 10) and NHES.S3.12.1 (owned in unit 4); owns no indicator. Leave-out note: the course still teaches boundaries and consent in unit 10 and access to care in unit 4; leaving it out removes HECAT.SH.HBO1 to HBO10 for the course. Serves HECAT.SH.HBO1 to HBO10. Curio Class writes: leave-out note, longer family note (English and Spanish), scenario cards, a services card. Cost: $3 | After units 4 and 10, whose skills it practices. Sensitive units sit last, before the end-of-course check, which never samples them; if left out, its four days extend unit 10 | Candidate: HECAT SH module 9 to 12 read (the module was read in part 2026-11-16) may add expectations on reproductive rights and state laws; one line naming that the school adds its state's rules | Sensitive. Family note two weeks before with the outline and how to opt out through the school (a student 18 or over decides for themselves; at home the parent decides). Medically accurate, inclusive of every orientation and gender identity, no shame, no scare images, no pictures of bodies, dated sources. No student is asked about their own relationships or activity. Teacher's own views note. Disclosure steps (APPROACH.md). Agent reads: sensitivity (LGBTQ students, students of every faith and culture, students who are parents; the HECAT 2021 Sexual Health module 9 to 12 expectations and CDC adolescent sexual health pages) |
235: | 20 | Sexual violence, abuse and dating violence (sensitive, removable). *What is abuse in a relationship, and how do I get help for myself or a friend?* | 3 | g9-v-abuse-and-dating-violence | | Communicating (standard 4): boundary steps and conflict steps practiced, with help-steps (no new skill) | Day 1: sexual violence, abuse and dating violence, including coercion and digital abuse; what consent means; warning signs; it is never the fault of the one harmed. Day 2: how to get help for yourself or a friend, and how not to take part (a bystander line), with written refusal and help lines only; help lines named by purpose (a national dating abuse help line, the national sexual assault hotline, the child abuse hotline). Day 3: end-of-unit check on unseen scenarios: name the warning sign and write the help step. Practices NHES.S4.12.4 and NHES.S4.12.6 (both owned in unit 10); owns no indicator. Leave-out note: the course still teaches limits, consent and conflict in unit 10 and getting help in unit 8. Serves HECAT.V.HBO4, HBO8, HBO9. Curio Class writes: leave-out note, longer family note, scripts, help-line card. Cost: $2 | After unit 10's boundary and conflict steps and unit 19. Last before unit 21 and the end-of-course check, which never samples it; if left out, its three days extend unit 10 | Candidate: HECAT V module 9 to 12 read may add sexting and image-sharing laws; one line with the school's state rule | Sensitive. Leave-out note, family note and the 18-or-over rule as in unit 19. No student is asked about their own experience; no role play of a person who harms; a card plays any pressure. A counselor knows the dates. Disclosure steps: listen, do not promise to keep it secret, follow the school's duty to report, tell the student who the next adult is. Agent reads: sensitivity (students who have been harmed; CDC teen dating violence prevention pages and a national sexual assault organization's guidance for schools) |
236: | 21 | Suicide and self-harm: getting help (sensitive, removable). *What are the warning signs, and what do I do if a friend may be thinking about suicide?* | 3 | g9-meh-suicide-and-self-harm-help | | Communicating (standard 4): help-steps and the concern response practiced (no new skill) | The help line and an adult plan are on the first page. Day 1: warning signs; what to say to a friend (ask directly, listen, stay, do not keep it secret); who to tell right away; 988 by call, text or chat. Day 2: written-script practice of the ask and the telling line, and where help is at school and nearby. Day 3: end-of-unit check on unseen scenarios: name a warning sign and write the help step. No method detail. Practices NHES.S4.12.3 and NHES.S4.12.10 (both owned in unit 8); owns no indicator. Leave-out note: the course still teaches asking for and giving help and showing concern in unit 8. Serves HECAT.MEH.HBO6; V.HBO10. Curio Class writes: leave-out note, longer family note, scripts, help card. Cost: $2 | After unit 8's help and concern, which it extends; last before the end-of-course check, which never samples it; if left out, its three days extend unit 8 | Candidate: HECAT MEH module 9 to 12 read may add self-harm help-seeking; kept to the help step | Sensitive. Leave-out note, family note and the 18-or-over rule as in unit 19. Safe-messaging rules: no method detail, no stories of a death, no "it was the only way" framing; the counselor is on call on each day; students are not asked about their own feelings; a student who is distressed during the unit is cared for under the school's plan. Disclosure steps as in unit 20. Agent reads: sensitivity (students who have lost someone to suicide or are struggling now; SAMHSA, "Preventing Suicide: A Toolkit for High Schools", and the 988 Lifeline's safe-messaging pages) |

FILE docs/courses/world-geography-and-ancient-civilizations-i/frame.md
136: | 1 | How we study the past. How can we know anything about people who lived thousands of years ago? | 10 | ss6-social-science-lenses; ss6-timeline-deep-time | MA.6.T1.1; MA.6.T1.3; D1.1.6-8; MA.6.T2.8; MA.6.T2.1 | D1.1.6-8 | The five social sciences and the kind of question each asks, sorted and explained on one site (such as Ur), and one worked investigation where the specialists combine their evidence to test an idea (MA.6.T1.3), about 3 days; the timeline from centuries to millions of years (era, millennium, BCE and CE, circa, no year zero), drawn to scale on a long strip and a zoomed strip, about 4 days; last, about 3 days: Ice Ages, changing coastlines and the greening and drying of the Sahara, and how they let people spread, as two objectives with their own checks, with grade 4's Beringia land bridge as the worked case and the out-of-Africa map as the last day's question, which unit 2 opens. | The year opens with how we study the past and with geography, and every later unit names the fields its evidence comes from and adds to this timeline. Grade 4's to-scale timeline returns with a retrieval task. Math 6 opens negative numbers on this timeline. | Candidate: none beyond the anchor; California 6.1 and New York's grade 6 opening match. | Natural, slow climate change only; today's warming and its human cause are grade 7 science. Keep the span arithmetic to counting across the BCE and CE line. Sensitivity read. |
137: | 2 | Early humans and the first farmers. Did the turn to farming make life better? | 15 | ss6-changing-interpretations; ss6-paleolithic-neolithic; ss6-complex-societies | MA.6.T1.2; MA.6.T2.2; D1.2.6-8; D2.His.9.6-8; MA.6.T2.3; MA.6.T2.4; MA.6.T2.5; D2.His.2.6-8; MA.6.T2.6; MA.6.T2.7 | D1.2.6-8; D2.His.9.6-8; D2.His.2.6-8 | Where modern humans arose in Africa and how they spread, as a case of an interpretation changing with new finds (Jebel Irhoud) and of how far experts agree, each secondary account sourced before accounts are compared; Paleolithic life and the Neolithic turn to farming in several places on their own, with Catalhoyuk as the worked town; three ways of farming life still followed today; the seven traits of a complex society as a debated working definition and how societies spread and meet. Lettered parts, all taught here: MA.6.T2.6a (food surplus), MA.6.T2.6b (dense populations, cities), MA.6.T2.6c (stratified classes), MA.6.T2.6d (specialized work), MA.6.T2.6e (government, religion, learning), MA.6.T2.6f (technology, art, monuments), MA.6.T2.6g (written or oral record keeping). | In time order, after the timeline exists to hold it. Interpretation is taught on the deep past because the sources (fossils, tools, dating, genetics) show most plainly how a view is revised. The trait checklist is needed by units 4 to 11. | Candidate: placed, Catalhoyuk as the worked Neolithic town (inside MA.6.T2.4; open museum and excavation images), Jericho a named example. | Human origins taught from the scientific record (values rule b); some families hold faith accounts; no student states a belief. Use "complex society", count oral record keeping, never rank peoples. Sensitivity read. |
138: | 3 | The land and peoples of Western Asia, the Middle East and North Africa today. Why do people live where they do in a dry land? (The action days' supporting question: what should we do about water where we live?) | 14 | ss6-mena-maps; ss6-mena-settlement | MA.6.T3a.1; MA.6.T3a.2; MA.6.T3a.3; D2.Geo.2.6-8; D2.Geo.8.6-8 | D2.Geo.2.6-8; D2.Geo.8.6-8 | Physical and political maps of the region (rivers, seas, straits, canal; countries, capitals, cities); absolute location in degrees, east and west of the prime meridian, read with directions, not signs, and relative location by direction and scale; why people live by rivers, coasts and oases; climate graphs (rainfall bars and temperature points read separately, then compared); oil and water as resources; environmental change seen from satellite images (the Mesopotamian marshes, the Nile delta). The year's action cycle (about 4 of the 14 days, one held for the look-back at the start of unit 5). | The anchor puts the region's geography before its ancient history (6.T3a before 6.T3b), and the map tools and the regional frame are used in every later unit. Science 6 (climate and latitude) and math 6 (four quadrants) lean on this unit, so it sits early. | Candidate: none beyond the anchor. | Present-day countries named as they are today; the present-day conflicts of the region are not this unit's subject. Climate graph axes explained before any comparison. The action asks no student to share family water or energy use. Sensitivity read. |
139: | 4 | Mesopotamia. Who gained most from the first cities, and who paid for them? | 16 | ss6-mesopotamia-states; ss6-surplus-specialization; ss6-mesopotamia-society; ss6-hammurabi-code | MA.6.T3b.1; MA.6.T3b.2; MA.6.T3b.3; D2.Eco.1.6-8; D2.Eco.13.6-8; MA.6.T3b.4; MA.6.T3b.4a; MA.6.T3b.4b; MA.6.T3b.4c; MA.6.T3b.4d; MA.6.T3b.4e; D2.His.6.6-8; D2.His.16.6-8; D2.Civ.12.6-8 | D2.Eco.1.6-8; D2.Eco.13.6-8; D2.His.6.6-8; D2.His.16.6-8; D2.Civ.12.6-8 | Floods, irrigation and the Fertile Crescent; Sumer, Babylon and Assyria read from a dated site map and the timeline; how irrigation, mining for copper and tin, metalworking, animal domestication and tools raised productivity and freed specialists, and who gained less; Mesopotamian society checked against the traits; cuneiform, short Gilgamesh passages, and the Code of Hammurabi, with its "eye for an eye" principle applied differently by class, read as a ruler's source; one law and a revision students propose judged against the same public problem (D2.Civ.12); a short written claim: was the code just, and for whom? | First ancient society in the usual order (6.T3b), and it needs unit 3's maps and unit 2's traits. Hammurabi is the year's first full use of the sourcing questions on an ancient source, at grade 5's depth (grade 5's sourcing questions are in use from unit 1), which units 5, 8 and 11 build on. | Candidate: none beyond the anchor; California 6.2 matches. | Slavery (war captives, debt) named plainly; no role play. Penalties that differ by class are discussed, not enacted. Sensitivity read (historians of ancient slavery studied first). Partial base: Oak Sumer unit. |
140: | 5 | Egypt and Kush. Did the Nile make Egypt, and what can we know of Kush from records others wrote? | 15 | ss6-egypt-nile; ss6-egypt-society; ss6-egypt-achievements; ss6-nubia-kush | MA.6.T3c.1; MA.6.T3c.2; MA.6.T3c.4; MA.6.T3c.5; MA.6.T3c.6; MA.6.T3c.7; MA.6.T3c.3; D2.His.10.6-8 | D2.His.10.6-8 | The Nile flowing north (Upper Egypt in the south), floods, silt and the calendar; pharaohs, dynasties and one ruler studied closely; the social order and religion, with an age-suited source task linking the conduct Egyptians expected to their beliefs (MA.6.T3c.6); Old, Middle and New Kingdoms and their achievements, the Rosetta Stone; Kush from Egyptian records, Kush's own monuments and partly read script; kandake queens and the 25th dynasty. Perspective on royal art; corroboration on Kush. | Follows Mesopotamia in the anchor (6.T3c) and uses its sourcing questions; it sets up corroboration of a record written by outsiders, which grade 7 needs for the Indus. Closes the first third, so course-level check 1 follows it. | Candidate: none beyond the anchor; California 6.2 (Egypt and Kush) matches. | Pyramids built mainly by paid and conscripted Egyptian workers (Giza workers' town), and slavery in Egypt still named plainly. Nubian peoples in the present tense. Sensitivity read (Nubian community organizations' own pages studied first). Partial base: Oak Ancient Egypt unit. Opens with the look-back day held from unit 3. |
141: | 6 | Phoenicia and the alphabet. Which mattered more to the world, Phoenicia's ships or its letters? | 10 | ss6-phoenician-trade; ss6-writing-systems | MA.6.T3d.1; MA.6.T3d.2; D2.Eco.3.6-8; D2.Eco.5.6-8; D2.Eco.7.6-8; D2.Geo.11.6-8; MA.6.T3d.3; D2.Geo.7.6-8 | D2.Eco.3.6-8; D2.Eco.5.6-8; D2.Eco.7.6-8; D2.Geo.11.6-8; D2.Geo.7.6-8 | The ancient Mediterranean on a map; why Phoenicia's coast and cedar made it a sea-trading power; colonies such as Carthage; buyers, sellers, merchants who pool money and share risk; from barter to weighed silver to coins (personal finance touch, no added day: money as a store of value, so people can save for later, retrieving grade 3's saving); who did the work (paid sailors and craftworkers, enslaved people in households and workshops, and how far rowers were free or enslaved is uncertain); three ways of writing compared and how traders carried the alphabet toward Greek and Latin, with what changed and what stayed. | Next in the anchor (6.T3d). A short retrieval task on money (grade 3, used in grade 5) opens its money day. It sets up exchange and scripts that unit 8 traces across the region and grade 7 carries to the Silk Roads and Latin. | Candidate: none beyond the anchor. | Slavery named plainly where documented. Sensitivity read (historians of ancient slavery studied first). |
142: | 7 | Judaism, Christianity and Islam at their origins. How much did these three faiths share at their beginnings, and where did they differ? | 15 | ss6-ancient-israel; ss6-christianity-origins; ss6-islam-origins | MA.6.T3e.1; MA.6.T3e.2; MA.6.T3e.3; MA.6.T3e.3a; MA.6.T3e.3b; MA.6.T3e.3c; MA.6.T3e.3d; MA.6.T3f.1; MA.6.T3f.2; MA.6.T3f.3 | None owned; uses sourcing (D2.His.10 from unit 5) as review on scripture as a source | The region on a historical map; the Hebrew Bible's account of Abraham, Moses, the tribes' route from Mesopotamia to Canaan traced on the map, the kingdom and the two temples, beside what archaeology shows; Judaism's ideas; Christianity's rise from Judaism, the New Testament and its spread in the Roman Empire; Mecca, Medina, Muhammad's life and teachings (one God, the Five Pillars, honesty and charity), the hijra and the Qur'an, each attributed to Islamic sources; how the three relate as faiths that honor Abraham. Jewish, Christian and Muslim communities today. | The anchor groups the region's faiths (6.T3e, 6.T3f) after its early societies; Islam's origins (about 600 CE) are out of time order on purpose so the three are taught together, and grade 9 picks up Islam's spread after 632. | Candidate: how Judaism continued after 70 CE (the synagogue and rabbinic learning), named in California 6.3. | Religion taught as history; each scripture named as its followers' account; no devotional practice asked. No images of Muhammad on Curio Class pages. Place names used as historians use them for each period; the present-day conflict is not this unit's subject. Roman persecution named plainly. Sensitivity read (each faith community's own educational pages studied first). |
143: | 8 | Connections and contributions. Why does an ancient society still matter today? | 14 | ss6-silk-roads-exchange; ss6-research-contribution | MA.6.T3g.1; D2.His.1.6-8; MA.6.T3g.2; D1.3.6-8; D1.4.6-8; D1.5.6-8; D3.1.6-8; D2.His.3.6-8 | D2.His.1.6-8; D1.3.6-8; D1.4.6-8; D1.5.6-8; D3.1.6-8; D2.His.3.6-8 | How trade, exchange and conquest linked the region (the Silk Roads and sea routes, Persian roads, Alexander), with one event traced through its causes and effects (about 5 days); the year's first research project: supporting questions and where experts agree on them, choosing sources that show differing viewpoints (at least one primary and one secondary), testing each at the 6 to 8 depth for its origin, structure, context, and whether other sources agree, revising the question, and a short written report arguing why one contribution is significant, with a source list (about 9 of the 14 days). | Closes the anchor's region (6.T3g), drawing on units 4 to 7 for topics. The research project needs the sourcing questions of units 4 and 5 and claim and evidence from units 2 and 4. Course-level check 2 follows it. | Candidate: none beyond the anchor. | Students may choose a faith's contribution: the report argues historical significance, never religious truth. Jewish, Christian and Muslim contributions are attributed accurately and shown with their internal diversity, using unit 7's community sources first (days 105 to 111). Sensitivity read. |
