## Pattern notes for the other PE frames

- Compute the short path from the band's own dose: (sessions less the short target) Fluency days a year, capped per
  unit.
- Before naming a model or placing food, water, calm, rules, conflict or a cultural game, read the same grades'
  health, SEL, arts and dance frames; where one teaches first in the year, open with retrieval and teach only PE's
  part, in their words.
- Across a year's two checks, sample every standard from units before the check; place S4 rows before the
  year-end check, moving days only inside the window the check weeks fix.
- Every unit's Ends: one skill task (a reviewed cue in concept units), one knowledge prompt, one S3 behavior.
- "Begun toward" only for a later owner; "used in new settings" for an earlier one.

They follow the order of a unit: plan, teach, practise, check, and then two that run through all of it.

**1. Start from what students know, and teach in an order where each idea rests on the last.**
New ideas are learned by connecting them to what is already in memory, and what a student already believes can
also mislead (How People Learn II, conclusion 5-1; Deans for Impact). These are summaries of a large body of
research, not a tested recipe for ordering a course. *Rule:* a section plan names the earlier standards it builds
on and checks them first; the scope and sequence gives "builds on" and "where it comes back" for every unit.

**2. Know it, do it, use it, together.**
Students learn why an idea works, become accurate and quick with it, and use it. None of the three waits for
the others (Deans for Impact; the math page gives that subject's sources). *Rule:* each subject page names the
kinds of day it balances, and a section plan marks every day as one of them.

**3. Teach new things clearly, in small steps, with models. Then take the help away.**
Working memory is small, and for beginners clear teaching beats partial guidance (Rosenshine 2012; Clark,
Kirschner and Sweller 2012, which is an argued position). Alternating worked examples with problems is rated
moderate (Pashler et al. 2007, recommendation 2). That rating is for math and science problems. In writing,
teaching strategies directly, with the teacher modelling and then handing over, is rated strong for elementary
grades and for grades 6 to 12 (WWC elementary and secondary writing guides). In reading, teaching comprehension
strategies the same way is rated strong for kindergarten to grade 3 and, in an older guide, for grades 4 to 12.
Each rating is for the whole practice, with modelling as one part. Modelling the close reading of a single text
is common practice with no rating. As skill grows the advantage of
examples reverses, so the help must fade. *Rule:* a section plan names who holds the model on each day and the day
it is withdrawn. A teach tool shows a model before it asks.

**4. A lesson may open with a short task. The teaching follows the same day.**
This is a real debate, and we do not pretend it is settled. Research does not support only student-centered or
only teacher-directed teaching (National Mathematics Advisory Panel, finding 23). A 2021 review of 53 studies
found that trying a problem before being taught helped older students understand ideas, on average, and did
nothing for procedures. For grades 2 to 5 the few comparisons pointed the other way: teaching first did
slightly better. In every comparison both groups were taught; only the order changed (Sinha and Kapur 2021). A
widely used open math course puts a task before the explanation from kindergarten up (Illustrative Mathematics
K-5), and science standards are built on starting from something to explain. *Rule:* an opening task is allowed
when students already have what they need to attempt it and the goal is an idea, not a procedure; in math, ten
minutes at most. In grades 2 to 5, teaching first is the default for anything new, unless the subject page says
otherwise and gives its source. A section plan says how each day opens and for how long.

**5. Talk and writing are part of learning.**
Asking students to explain why and how is rated strong (Pashler et al. 2007, recommendation 7); that supports
"why" questions, not every kind of discussion. For English learners, building spoken and written language into
content lessons is rated strong, and regular structured writing is recommended on minimal evidence (WWC English
learner guide). *Rule:* a section plan names two or three planned questions and the written task. A tool cannot
judge open writing; a teacher does, with a rubric and sample pieces on paper (FOUNDATION).

**6. Choose the medium for the idea: hands, paper or screen.**
Linking concrete and abstract forms of an idea is rated moderate; the same guide warns that an idea learned
only with objects can stay stuck to them, so the objects are faded out on a named day (Pashler et al. 2007,
recommendation 4). On screens the evidence is mixed. Practice and tutorial software has generally helped in
studies, and one large national study found no gain when schools used it in the ordinary way (National
Mathematics Advisory Panel, finding 28), so we do not count on software to teach by itself. No technology is
right everywhere (How People Learn II, conclusions 8-1, 8-2). In one interview study with college students,
people learned from changing a simulation themselves and little from watching it (Adams et al. 2008). *Rule:* a
tool is built only when a section plan names its job, and each use of it has one of three jobs (one tool may be used
for two, such as a teach tool that shows why and then checks unaided; the plan names the part, the job and the
minutes): **show why** (the student
predicts, changes something, sees what follows), **practise with feedback**, or **check unaided**. A tool does
not replace the teacher's explanation, hold a discussion, judge open work, stand in for real materials, or know
a student from one day to the next. The plan says what is done by hand, with what supplies.

**7. Practise over time, mixed, and from memory.**
Spacing is rated moderate and quizzing with feedback strong (Pashler et al. 2007, recommendations 1 and 5b); a
review of study techniques rated the same two highest, and mixing problem types promising but less certain
(Dunlosky 2013). *Rule:* a section plan says what earlier work is practised in it and where its own ideas come
back. How (a warm-up, a notebook, a return to a text) is set by the subject page.

**8. Every day leaves evidence the teacher can act on.**
Checking helps when the evidence changes the teaching, and feedback helps when it is about the work and the
next step, not a mark (Black and Wiliam 1998; Leahy et al. 2005). Feedback varies widely in effect and can harm
(EEF feedback report). *Rule:* a unit's end-of-unit assessment is written before its days. Each item names the common
wrong answers, what each shows, and what to do if many students made it. The form of the daily evidence is set
by the subject page: an exit question in math; the notebook and the class model in science. What FOUNDATION
costs us: no accounts means no live dashboard. The teacher holds the record, on score codes and a printed class
sheet, and that is slower.

**9. Every learner works on grade-level content, with supports built into the lesson.**
Teaching a small set of academic words in depth is rated strong (WWC English learner guide, recommendation 1).
Guidance for English learners asks that language support sit inside the lesson and the challenge be kept (ELSF;
WIDA 2020). Universal Design for Learning asks for more than one way in, more than one way to respond (CAST
2024); we treat it as a design framework, not a proven method. *Rule:* every section plan fills three hooks: an
unaided check, English learner supports, and access. Extension goes deeper on the same standard. For each
readiness gap the plan gives a ten-minute reteach and the earlier-grade tool or page. Curio Class is not an
intervention program, and a student two or more years behind needs more than it offers.

**10. Motivation comes from success and purpose.**
Students work harder when they feel they belong, have some control and see themselves improving; rewards for
performing can lower interest, though findings are mixed (How People Learn II, chapter 6). Decoration that is
not about the idea hurts learning (Deans for Impact). *Rule:* pictures and stories in a tool serve the idea;
practice starts where most students succeed and climbs; feedback is about the task and what to try next, never
about the child.

# Cory's decision baseline

Cory's standing rules for deciding questions in Curio Class, drawn from his answers on the decision board (raw
answers: `answers.json`, id -> choice and note; questions: `decisions.json`) and from his later decisions in
DESK-ANSWERS.md. Rule numbers are cited across the repository and never change.

Every spec, build and review agent reads this before deciding anything itself. Settle questions with these rules.
Bring Cory only questions these rules cannot settle and that matter (see "What goes to Cory").

## The rules

1. **Quality over saved work.** Never merge, shorten or share something only to save effort. Spans, shared engines and
   shared figures are fine only when nothing is lost for any grade or subject. If something is lost, build it
   separately (gf-04, w1-21).
2. **Every standard is taught well, above the minimum.** A code on a topic must be really taught in that tool. If it
   cannot be taught well there, remove it from the topic and make sure another tool teaches it well. Never add a thin
   activity just to make a code honest (gf-07, w2-01, w2-21, w2-22, w1-15).
3. **Better graphics by default.** When there is a choice between drawn-in-code placeholders and real illustrated art,
   choose the better art (w1-42).
4. **Every tool stands alone.** The site is public and a student, parent or young child may open any tool with no
   teacher present. Defaults must work for a solo user; teacher toggles are extras, never the main experience.
   Example: in fair-use-review, the student makes their own call first and Theo's call shows after (w1-53, w1-30, w1-31).
5. **Facts stay plain, with the human side.** Don't soften history or science. Present it with care for the people
   involved. Sensitivity and accuracy checks on cultural, Native or hard-history content, in a tool or a course, are
   done by a fresh sensitivity agent; once that read passes, the content can go live (proj-03). No outside reader is
   looked for. The sensitivity agent reads every unit after studying the community's own published voice and the
   scholarship (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md), and we teach with honesty and dignity at an age-appropriate
   level (settled 2026-11-14, see DESK-ANSWERS).
6. **Classroom-safe language.** No slurs or expletives. A grawlix (#@$!) stands in where a curse would be (w1-28).
7. **Privacy is stated.** Wherever a tool records or stores anything, say on screen that it never leaves the device
   (w1-20).
8. **Teacher notes name where the standards cover sensitive topics** (for example, dating in the 6-8 health standards)
   (w1-46).
9. **Lean toward the real-world factor.** Show the realistic version by default (the carbon price slider is on by
   default in grid-operator) (w3-33).
10. **Good ideas become tools.** When a question surfaces a new tool idea, add it to the concept pipeline for a later
    spec wave. Queued: "fact-check a chatbot" (generative AI, from ai-evaluator); a resume builder, near term (w1-02,
    w2-12).
11. **People art is drawn, and checked.** Codex may draw people. Every people asset carries `"people": true`, every
    people image is on a contact sheet Cory sees before the tool ships, and a fresh agent checks each set for
    stereotypes and representation. Reviews add people where a scene needs them.
12. **2 MB is a goal, not a blocker.** Over 6 MB, notes.md says why in a `Size:` line (`scripts/check_tools.py`
    enforces only that).
13. **Shared code is grown, not planned.** A kit engine is promoted only after three finished tools use the same code.
    Older tools pick it up later and only when needed: they work today, and the gain is efficiency, not capability.
14. **Finish before scaling.** At most about 10 Codex tools built and not yet reviewed. Finish a small cohort end to
    end before a new wave, and log repeat problems in the issue ledger.
15. **Public copy names no companies.** The ambition in docs/VISION.md (replace paid curriculum) stays inside the
    vision; the site says "a free, complete classroom toolkit".
16. **The first tap may cut the welcome.** A tool does not have to let its welcome line finish after the student's
    first tap; acting at once beats listening. Specs, builds and reviews do not flag a welcome cut short by a tap.
    What the welcome told the student must still be reachable (the on-screen hint or Read), and a line the tool needs
    later (a premise, a role) is said again where it is needed, not only in the welcome.
17. **Curiosities finish on Cory's desk review.** Span and Spire, Pocket Universe and later Curiosities get Cory's
    final review on the Review Desk; plate wording (the track was renamed from Instruments to Curiosities on 2026-11-05) and any areas to lift are noted
    there and fixed after it, not before. Expect larger design work back on the first two.
18. **Curiosities ship together.** The home page Curiosities entrance is built after every planned Curiosity is fully
    built. Each Curiosity's voice goes on the Forge as it passes its exit check, added to the end of the running Forge
    queue; they are not held to be recorded together.
19. **Curiosities may keep a little game essence.** Don't be too strict about removing gamification. A Curiosity must
    stay purely fun and exploratory; a light game touch (a challenge to try, a surprise, a setup to break) is fine
    when it adds to that. A goal ladder, a grade, a forced finish or a game-over still does not belong. Lenses flag a
    game element only when it narrows play or turns exploring into scoring.
20. **State adoption is the bar we build to.** Full-course materials (units, Tracks, the Binder and the shelves that
    feed them) are built to meet the substance of state and national curriculum reviews. We do not pursue formal
    adoption now; if later data says it is a good path, we pivot and are already well placed. The bar never overrides
    "What we will not do" in docs/VISION.md (no accounts, tracking or student analytics). What the bar asks, what we
    took and what we left out: docs/ADOPTION-BAR.md.
21. **A tool teaches a standard only when it passes the teaching test.** A student alone, following the tool's lead,
    meets a model, does the standard's own verb (solve, write, explain, record) on every part of its wording, on five
    or more items that differ, with a check where thinking is cheaper than guessing, and none of it can be skipped.
    For writing and talk a tool cannot mark, a model, a guide while working and a self-check on screen against a shown
    guide count. Every tool states a role: `teach` (passes the test), `see why` (lets a student explore the idea and
    names what the unit adds) or `practice`. A tool claims a code only for work on its main path; a readout, an
    optional mode or a teacher note never carries a code. Recess games and Curiosities take the role `recess` or
    `curiosity` and claim no code.
22. **Topics name standards.** A topic page covers one teachable topic. It is split only when it holds two unrelated
    topics, and then properly: a split script, redirects for the old pages, and its Word Wall, chart and games divided
    by hand (its id keys all of them and about 762 live pages; docs/ECOSYSTEM.md). It is never split just to reach
    standard-level detail; the ledger does that. Split candidates come to Cory as exceptions. A topic's standards are

